[
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_ApexAssertionsShouldIncludeMessage",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Apex Assertions to Include Message",
    "description" : "Apex test assert statements should include a message parameter to provide additional context on test failures.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.13.0\n\nThe second parameter of System.assert/third parameter of System.assertEquals/System.assertNotEquals is a message.\nHaving a second/third parameter provides more information and makes it easier to debug the test failure and\nimproves the readability of test output.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n@isTest\npublic class Foo {\n    @isTest\n    static void methodATest() {\n        System.assertNotEquals('123', o.StageName); // not good\n        System.assertEquals('123', o.StageName, 'Opportunity stageName is wrong.'); // good\n        System.assert(o.isClosed); // not good\n        System.assert(o.isClosed, 'Opportunity is not closed.'); // good\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_ApexUnitTestClassShouldHaveAsserts",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Assertions in Apex Unit Test Classes",
    "description" : "Ensures that Apex unit test classes include at least one assertion such as System.assert(), assertEquals(), or assertNotEquals().",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.1\n\nApex unit tests should include at least one assertion.  This makes the tests more robust, and using assert\nwith messages provide the developer a clearer idea of what the test does.  Custom assert method invocation\npatterns can be specified using the 'additionalAssertMethodPattern' property if required.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n@isTest\npublic class Foo {\n    public static testMethod void testSomething() {\n        Account a = null;\n        // This is better than having a NullPointerException\n        // System.assertNotEquals(a, null, 'account not found');\n        a.toString();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_ApexUnitTestClassShouldHaveRunAs",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Apex Unit Test Classes to Include System.runAs",
    "description" : "Ensures Apex unit test classes contain at least one System.runAs() call to run code as a specific user.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.51.0\n\nApex unit tests should include at least one runAs method.  This makes the tests more robust, and independent from the \nuser running it.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n@isTest\nprivate class TestRunAs {\n   public static testMethod void testRunAs() {\n        // Setup test data\n        // Create a unique UserName\n        String uniqueUserName = 'standarduser' + DateTime.now().getTime() + '@testorg.com';\n        // This code runs as the system user\n        Profile p = [SELECT Id FROM Profile WHERE Name='Standard User'];\n        User u = new User(Alias = 'standt', Email='standarduser@testorg.com',\n        EmailEncodingKey='UTF-8', LastName='Testing', LanguageLocaleKey='en_US',\n        LocaleSidKey='en_US', ProfileId = p.Id,\n        TimeZoneSidKey='America/Los_Angeles',\n         UserName=uniqueUserName);\n\n        System.runAs(u) {\n              // The following code runs as user 'u'\n              System.debug('Current User: ' + UserInfo.getUserName());\n              System.debug('Current Profile: ' + UserInfo.getProfileId());\n          }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_ApexUnitTestMethodShouldHaveIsTestAnnotation",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce @isTest Annotation on Apex Unit Test Methods",
    "description" : "Ensures that Apex unit test methods use the @isTest annotation instead of the deprecated testMethod keyword.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.13.0\n\nApex test methods should have `@isTest` annotation instead of the `testMethod` keyword,\nas `testMethod` is deprecated.\nSalesforce advices to use [@isTest](https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.apexcode.meta/apexcode/apex_classes_annotation_isTest.htm)\nannotation for test classes and methods.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n@isTest\nprivate class ATest {\n    @isTest\n    static void methodATest() {\n    }\n    static void methodBTest() {\n    }\n    @isTest static void methodCTest() {\n        System.assert(1==2);\n    }\n    static testmethod void methodCTest() {\n        System.debug('I am a debug statement');\n    }\n    private void fetchData() {\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_ApexUnitTestShouldNotUseSeeAllDataTrue",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using seeAllData=true in Apex Unit Tests",
    "description" : "This rule checks that Apex unit tests do not use @isTest(seeAllData=true).",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.1\n\nApex unit tests should not use @isTest(seeAllData=true) because it opens up the existing database data for unexpected modification by tests.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n@isTest(seeAllData = true)\npublic class Foo {\n    public static testMethod void testSomething() {\n        Account a = null;\n        // This is better than having a NullPointerException\n        // System.assertNotEquals(a, null, 'account not found');\n        a.toString();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_AvoidGlobalModifier",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Global Modifier",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding the use of the global modifier on classes and methods in Apex code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nGlobal classes should be avoided (especially in managed packages) as they can never be deleted or changed in signature. Always check twice if something needs to be global.\nMany interfaces (e.g. Batch) required global modifiers in the past but don't require this anymore. Don't lock yourself in.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nglobal class Unchangeable {\n    global UndeletableType unchangable(UndeletableType param) {\n        // ...\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_AvoidLogicInTrigger",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Logic In Trigger",
    "description" : "This rule discourages placing business logic directly inside Apex triggers.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nAs triggers do not allow methods like regular classes they are less flexible and suited to apply good encapsulation style.\nTherefore delegate the triggers work to a regular class (often called Trigger handler class).\n\nSee more here: &lt;https://developer.salesforce.com/page/Trigger_Frameworks_and_Apex_Trigger_Best_Practices&gt;\n\nExample(s):\n```\ntrigger Accounts on Account (before insert, before update, before delete, after insert, after update, after delete, after undelete) {\n    for(Account acc : Trigger.new) {\n        if(Trigger.isInsert) {\n            // ...\n        }\n\n        // ...\n\n        if(Trigger.isDelete) {\n            // ...\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_DebugsShouldUseLoggingLevel",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce System.debug Calls to Specify Logging Level",
    "description" : "Ensures that calls to System.debug specify a logging level parameter.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.18.0\n\nThe first parameter of System.debug, when using the signature with two parameters, is a LoggingLevel enum.\n\nHaving the Logging Level specified provides a cleaner log, and improves readability of it.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n@isTest\npublic class Foo {\n    @isTest\n    static void bar() {\n        System.debug('Hey this code executed.'); // not good\n        System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN, 'Hey, something might be wrong.'); // good\n        System.debug(LoggingLevel.DEBUG, 'Hey, something happened.'); // not good when on strict mode\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "strictMode",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "If true, mark statements that use the DEBUG enum of LoggingLevel."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_bestpractices_UnusedLocalVariable",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unused Local Variable",
    "description" : "Detects local variables that are declared and/or assigned but never used in the code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.23.0\n\nDetects when a local variable is declared and/or assigned but not used.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic Boolean bar(String z) {\n        String x = 'some string'; // not used\n\n        String y = 'some other string'; // used in the next line\n        return z.equals(y);\n    }\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_ClassNamingConventions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Class Naming Conventions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that class names follow a specified naming convention, typically Pascal case for Apex classes.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nConfigurable naming conventions for type declarations. This rule reports\n            type declarations which do not match the regex that applies to their\n            specific kind (e.g. enum or interface). Each regex can be configured on the PMD configuration file.\nCheck the [PMD documentation](https://pmd.github.io/pmd-6.55.0/pmd_rules_apex_codestyle.html#classnamingconventions) for more information.\n\n            By default this rule uses the standard Apex naming convention (Pascal case).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class FooClass { } // This is in pascal case, so it's ok\n\npublic class fooClass { } // This will be reported unless you change the regex\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_FieldDeclarationsShouldBeAtStart",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Field Declarations Before Method Declarations",
    "description" : "Ensures that field declarations appear before method declarations within a class.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.23.0\n\nField declarations should appear before method declarations within a class.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Foo {\n    public Integer someField; // good\n\n    public void someMethod() {\n    }\n\n    public Integer anotherField; // bad\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_FieldNamingConventions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Field Naming Conventions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces configurable naming conventions for field declarations in Apex code, ensuring field names match specified regular expressions based on their kind (e.g., constants, static fields, final fields).",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.15.0\n\nConfigurable naming conventions for field declarations. This rule reports variable declarations\n            which do not match the regex that applies to their specific kind ---e.g. constants (static final),\n            static field, final field. Each regex can be configured on the PMD configuration file.\nCheck the [PMD documentation](https://pmd.github.io/pmd-6.55.0/pmd_rules_apex_codestyle.html#fieldnamingconventions) for more information.\n\n            By default this rule uses the standard Apex naming convention (Camel case).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    Integer instanceField; // This is in camel case, so it's ok\n\n    Integer INSTANCE_FIELD; // This will be reported unless you change the regex\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_ForLoopsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce For Loops Use Braces",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that 'for' loops must always use curly braces to surround their body statements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.6.0\n\nAvoid using 'for' statements without using surrounding braces. If the code formatting or\nindentation is lost then it becomes difficult to separate the code being controlled\nfrom the rest.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nfor (int i = 0; i < 42; i++) // not recommended\n    foo();\n\nfor (int i = 0; i < 42; i++) { // preferred approach\n    foo();\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_FormalParameterNamingConventions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Formal Parameter Naming Conventions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces naming conventions for formal parameters in methods based on configurable regular expressions, ensuring parameters follow the expected naming style such as camelCase.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.15.0\n\nConfigurable naming conventions for formal parameters of methods.\n            This rule reports formal parameters which do not match the regex that applies to their\n            specific kind (e.g. method parameter, or final method parameter). Each regex can be configured on the PMD configuration file.\nCheck the [PMD documentation](https://pmd.github.io/pmd-6.55.0/pmd_rules_apex_codestyle.html#formalparameternamingconventions) for more information.\n\n            By default this rule uses the standard Apex naming convention (Camel case).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public bar(Integer methodParameter) { } // This is in camel case, so it's ok\n\n    public baz(Integer METHOD_PARAMETER) { } // This will be reported unless you change the regex\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_IfElseStmtsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce If-Else Statements to Use Braces",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that all if-else statements use curly braces to surround their controlled blocks.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.6.0\n\nAvoid using if..else statements without using surrounding braces. If the code formatting\nor indentation is lost then it becomes difficult to separate the code being controlled\nfrom the rest.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// this is OK\nif (foo) x++;\n\n// but this is not\nif (foo)\n    x = x+1;\nelse\n    x = x-1;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_IfStmtsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Braces for If Statements",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of curly braces around the code block of if statements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.6.0\n\nAvoid using if statements without using braces to surround the code block. If the code\nformatting or indentation is lost then it becomes difficult to separate the code being\ncontrolled from the rest.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nif (foo)    // not recommended\n    x++;\n\nif (foo) {  // preferred approach\n    x++;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_LocalVariableNamingConventions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Local Variable Naming Conventions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces naming conventions for local variables in Apex code according to configurable regular expressions, typically requiring camel case.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.15.0\n\nConfigurable naming conventions for local variable declarations.\n            This rule reports variable declarations which do not match the regex that applies to their\n            specific kind (e.g. local variable, or final local variable). Each regex can be configured on the PMD configuration file.\nCheck the [PMD documentation](https://pmd.github.io/pmd-6.55.0/pmd_rules_apex_codestyle.html#localvariablenamingconventions) for more information.\n\n            By default this rule uses the standard Apex naming convention (Camel case).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public Foo() {\n        Integer localVariable; // This is in camel case, so it's ok\n\n        Integer LOCAL_VARIABLE; // This will be reported unless you change the regex\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_MethodNamingConventions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Method Naming Conventions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces configurable naming conventions for method declarations in Apex code, ensuring method names match specified regular expressions based on their type (e.g., static or test methods).",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nConfigurable naming conventions for method declarations. This rule reports\n            method declarations which do not match the regex that applies to their\n            specific kind (e.g. static method, or test method). Each regex can be configured on the PMD configuration file.\nCheck the [PMD documentation](https://pmd.github.io/pmd-6.55.0/pmd_rules_apex_codestyle.html#methodnamingconventions) for more information.\n\n            By default this rule uses the standard Apex naming convention (Camel case).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void instanceMethod() { } // This is in camel case, so it's ok\n\n    public void INSTANCE_METHOD() { } // This will be reported unless you change the regex\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_OneDeclarationPerLine",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce One Declaration Per Line",
    "description" : "Enforces that each variable declaration is on its own line to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.7.0\n\nApex allows the use of several variables declaration of the same type on one line. However, it\ncan lead to quite messy code. This rule looks for several declarations on the same line.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nInteger a, b;   // not recommended\n\nInteger a,\n        b;      // ok by default, can be flagged setting the strictMode property\n\nInteger a;      // preferred approach\nInteger b;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "reportInForLoopInitializer",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "If false, multiple declarations in a for loop initializer are not flagged."
      },
      {
        "name" : "strictMode",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "If true, mark combined declaration even if the declarations are on separate lines."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_PropertyNamingConventions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Property Naming Conventions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces configurable naming conventions for property declarations in Apex code, ensuring property names match specified regex patterns based on their kind (e.g., static or instance properties).",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.15.0\n\nConfigurable naming conventions for property declarations. This rule reports\n            property declarations which do not match the regex that applies to their\n            specific kind (e.g. static property, or instance property). Each regex can be configured on the PMD configuration file.\nCheck the [PMD documentation](https://pmd.github.io/pmd-6.55.0/pmd_rules_apex_codestyle.html#propertynamingconventions) for more information.\n\n            By default this rule uses the standard Apex naming convention (Camel case).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public Integer instanceProperty { get; set; } // This is in camel case, so it's ok\n\n    public Integer INSTANCE_PROPERTY { get; set; } // This will be reported unless you change the regex\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_codestyle_WhileLoopsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Braces in While Loops",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of curly braces in while loops to clearly define the loop's code block.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.6.0\n\nAvoid using 'while' statements without using braces to surround the code block. If the code\nformatting or indentation is lost then it becomes difficult to separate the code being\ncontrolled from the rest.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nwhile (true)    // not recommended\n    x++;\n\nwhile (true) {  // preferred approach\n    x++;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_AvoidDeeplyNestedIfStmts",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Deeply Nested If Statements",
    "description" : "This rule flags deeply nested if-then statements that reduce code readability and maintainability.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nAvoid creating deeply nested if-then statements since they are harder to read and error-prone to maintain.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar(Integer x, Integer y, Integer z) {\n        if (x>y) {\n            if (y>z) {\n                if (z==x) {\n                    // !! too deep\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_CognitiveComplexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Limit Cognitive Complexity",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods with high cognitive complexity, which are difficult to read and maintain.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.22.0\n\nMethods that are highly complex are difficult to read and more costly to maintain. If you include too much decisional\nlogic within a single method, you make its behavior hard to understand and more difficult to modify.\n\nCognitive complexity is a measure of how difficult it is for humans to read and understand a method. Code that contains\na break in the control flow is more complex, whereas the use of language shorthands doesn't increase the level of\ncomplexity. Nested control flows can make a method more difficult to understand, with each additional nesting of the\ncontrol flow leading to an increase in cognitive complexity.\n\nInformation about Cognitive complexity can be found in the original paper here:\n&lt;https://www.sonarsource.com/docs/CognitiveComplexity.pdf&gt;\n\nBy default, this rule reports methods with a complexity of 15 or more. Reported methods should be broken down into less\ncomplex components.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    // Has a cognitive complexity of 0\n    public void createAccount() {\n        Account account = new Account(Name = 'PMD');\n        insert account;\n    }\n\n    // Has a cognitive complexity of 1\n    public Boolean setPhoneNumberIfNotExisting(Account a, String phone) {\n        if (a.Phone == null) {                          // +1\n            a.Phone = phone;\n            update a;\n            return true;\n        }\n\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    // Has a cognitive complexity of 4\n    public void updateContacts(List<Contact> contacts) {\n        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();\n\n        for (Contact contact : contacts) {                           // +1\n            if (contact.Department == 'Finance') {                   // +2 (nesting = 1)\n                contact.Title = 'Finance Specialist';\n                contactsToUpdate.add(contact);\n            } else if (contact.Department == 'Sales') {              // +1\n                contact.Title = 'Sales Specialist';\n                contactsToUpdate.add(contact);\n            }\n        }\n\n        update contactsToUpdate;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_CyclomaticComplexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Limit Cyclomatic Complexity",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods and classes with high cyclomatic complexity, which indicates too many decision points and complex control flow in code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nThe complexity of methods directly affects maintenance costs and readability. Concentrating too much decisional logic\nin a single method makes its behaviour hard to read and change.\n\nCyclomatic complexity assesses the complexity of a method by counting the number of decision points in a method,\nplus one for the method entry. Decision points are places where the control flow jumps to another place in the\nprogram. As such, they include all control flow statements, such as 'if', 'while', 'for', and 'case'.\n\nGenerally, numbers ranging from 1-4 denote low complexity, 5-7 denote moderate complexity, 8-10 denote\nhigh complexity, and 11+ is very high complexity. By default, this rule reports methods with a complexity &gt;= 10.\nAdditionally, classes with many methods of moderate complexity get reported as well once the total of their\nmethods' complexities reaches 40, even if none of the methods was directly reported.\n\nReported methods should be broken down into several smaller methods. Reported classes should probably be broken down\ninto subcomponents.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Complicated {\n  public void example() { // This method has a cyclomatic complexity of 12\n    int x = 0, y = 1, z = 2, t = 2;\n    boolean a = false, b = true, c = false, d = true;\n    if (a && b || b && d) {\n      if (y == z) {\n        x = 2;\n      } else if (y == t && !d) {\n        x = 2;\n      } else {\n        x = 2;\n      }\n    } else if (c && d) {\n      while (z < y) {\n        x = 2;\n      }\n    } else {\n      for (int n = 0; n < t; n++) {\n        x = 2;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_ExcessiveClassLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Class Length",
    "description" : "Detects classes that are excessively long and potentially overloaded with responsibilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nExcessive class file lengths are usually indications that the class may be burdened with excessive\nresponsibilities that could be provided by external classes or functions. In breaking these methods\napart the code becomes more managable and ripe for reuse.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar1() {\n        // 1000 lines of code\n    }\n    public void bar2() {\n        // 1000 lines of code\n    }\n    public void bar3() {\n        // 1000 lines of code\n    }\n    public void barN() {\n        // 1000 lines of code\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_ExcessiveParameterList",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Parameter List",
    "description" : "This rule discourages methods with long parameter lists to improve maintainability and readability.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nMethods with numerous parameters are a challenge to maintain, especially if most of them share the\nsame datatype. These situations usually denote the need for new objects to wrap the numerous parameters.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// too many arguments liable to be mixed up\npublic void addPerson(Integer birthYear, Integer birthMonth, Integer birthDate, Integer height, Integer weight, Integer ssn) {\n    // ...\n}\n// preferred approach\npublic void addPerson(Date birthdate, BodyMeasurements measurements, int ssn) {\n    // ...\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_ExcessivePublicCount",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Number of Public Methods and Attributes",
    "description" : "Detects classes with a large number of public methods and attributes.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nClasses with large numbers of public methods and attributes require disproportionate testing efforts\nsince combinational side effects grow rapidly and increase risk. Refactoring these classes into\nsmaller ones not only increases testability and reliability but also allows new variations to be\ndeveloped easily.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public String value;\n    public Bar something;\n    public Variable var;\n    // [... more more public attributes ...]\n\n    public void doWork() {}\n    public void doMoreWork() {}\n    public void doWorkAgain() {}\n    // [... more more public methods ...]\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_NcssConstructorCount",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Limit Constructor NCSS Line Count",
    "description" : "This rule checks that constructors do not exceed a specified number of Non-Commenting Source Statements (NCSS) lines, which measure the actual executable statements excluding comments.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nThis rule uses the NCSS (Non-Commenting Source Statements) algorithm to determine the number of lines\nof code for a given constructor. NCSS ignores comments, and counts actual statements. Using this algorithm,\nlines of code that are split are counted as one.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo extends Bar {\n    //this constructor only has 1 NCSS lines\n    public Foo() {\n        super();\n\n\n\n\n        super.foo();\n}\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_NcssMethodCount",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce NCSS Method Count Limit",
    "description" : "This rule checks that methods do not exceed a specified Non-Commenting Source Statements (NCSS) line count, which measures the number of executable statements excluding comments.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nThis rule uses the NCSS (Non-Commenting Source Statements) algorithm to determine the number of lines\nof code for a given method. NCSS ignores comments, and counts actual statements. Using this algorithm,\nlines of code that are split are counted as one.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo extends Bar {\n    //this method only has 1 NCSS lines\n    public Integer method() {\n        super.method();\n\n\n\n        return 1;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_NcssTypeCount",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Limit NCSS Type Count",
    "description" : "This rule checks that the number of Non-Commenting Source Statements (NCSS) in a type does not exceed a certain threshold.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nThis rule uses the NCSS (Non-Commenting Source Statements) algorithm to determine the number of lines\nof code for a given type. NCSS ignores comments, and counts actual statements. Using this algorithm,\nlines of code that are split are counted as one.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n//this class only has 6 NCSS lines\npublic class Foo extends Bar {\n    public Foo() {\n        super();\n\n\n\n\n\n        super.foo();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_StdCyclomaticComplexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Limit Cyclomatic Complexity",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods with cyclomatic complexity exceeding standard thresholds, indicating overly complex methods.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nComplexity directly affects maintenance costs is determined by the number of decision points in a method\nplus one for the method entry.  The decision points include 'if', 'while', 'for', and 'case labels' calls.\nGenerally, numbers ranging from 1-4 denote low complexity, 5-7 denote moderate complexity, 8-10 denote\nhigh complexity, and 11+ is very high complexity.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// This has a Cyclomatic Complexity = 12\npublic class Foo {\n1   public void example() {\n2   if (a == b || (c == d && e == f)) {\n3       if (a1 == b1) {\n            fiddle();\n4       } else if a2 == b2) {\n            fiddle();\n        }  else {\n            fiddle();\n        }\n5   } else if (c == d) {\n6       while (c == d) {\n            fiddle();\n        }\n7   } else if (e == f) {\n8       for (int n = 0; n < h; n++) {\n            fiddle();\n        }\n    } else {\n        switch (z) {\n9           case 1:\n                fiddle();\n                break;\n10          case 2:\n                fiddle();\n                break;\n11          case 3:\n                fiddle();\n                break;\n12          default:\n                fiddle();\n                break;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_design_TooManyFields",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Too Many Fields in a Class",
    "description" : "Detects classes that have an excessive number of fields, which can make the class unwieldy and hard to maintain.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nClasses that have too many fields can become unwieldy and could be redesigned to have fewer fields,\npossibly through grouping related fields in new objects.  For example, a class with individual\ncity/state/zip fields could park them within a single Address field.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Person {\n    // too many separate fields\n    Integer birthYear;\n    Integer birthMonth;\n    Integer birthDate;\n    Double height;\n    Double weight;\n}\n\npublic class Person {\n    // this is more manageable\n    Date birthDate;\n    BodyMeasurements measurements;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_documentation_ApexDoc",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce ApexDoc Comments",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that ApexDoc comments are present and correctly formatted for classes, methods, and properties according to visibility and configuration settings.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.8.0\n\nThis rule validates that:\n\n*   ApexDoc comments are present for classes, methods, and properties that are public or global, excluding\n    overrides and test classes (as well as the contents of test classes).\n*   ApexDoc comments are present for classes, methods, and properties that are protected or private, depending\n    on the properties `reportPrivate` and `reportProtected`.\n*   ApexDoc comments should contain @description depending on the property `reportMissingDescription`.\n*   ApexDoc comments on non-void, non-constructor methods should contain @return.\n*   ApexDoc comments on void or constructor methods should not contain @return.\n*   ApexDoc comments on methods with parameters should contain @param for each parameter, in the same\n    order as the method signature.\n*   ApexDoc comments are present on properties is only validated, if the property `reportProperty` is enabled.\n    By setting `reportProperty` to false, you can ignore missing comments on properties.\n\nMethod overrides and tests are both exempted from having ApexDoc.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n/**\n * @description Hello World\n */\npublic class HelloWorld {\n    /**\n     * @description Bar\n     * @return Bar\n     */\n    public Object bar() { return null; }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_ApexCSRF",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid DML Operations in Apex Class Constructors or Initializers",
    "description" : "This rule detects and prevents DML operations in Apex class constructors, initializers, or any method named 'init'.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nHaving DML operations in Apex class constructor or initializers can have unexpected side effects:\nBy just accessing a page, the DML statements would be executed and the database would be modified.\nJust querying the database is permitted.\n\nIn addition to constructors and initializers, any method called `init` is checked as well.\n\nSalesforce Apex already protects against this scenario and raises a runtime exception.\n\nNote: This rule has been moved from category &quot;Security&quot; to &quot;Error Prone&quot; with PMD 6.21.0, since\nusing DML in constructors is not a security problem, but crashes the application.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    // initializer\n    {\n        insert data;\n    }\n\n    // static initializer\n    static {\n        insert data;\n    }\n\n    // constructor\n    public Foo() {\n        insert data;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_AvoidDirectAccessTriggerMap",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Direct Access to Trigger Maps",
    "description" : "This rule prevents directly accessing Trigger.old and Trigger.new maps in Apex triggers to avoid bugs related to improper bulk handling.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nAvoid directly accessing Trigger.old and Trigger.new as it can lead to a bug. Triggers should be bulkified and iterate through the map to handle the actions for each item separately.\n\nExample(s):\n```\ntrigger AccountTrigger on Account (before insert, before update) {\n   Account a = Trigger.new[0]; //Bad: Accessing the trigger array directly is not recommended.\n\n   for ( Account a : Trigger.new ) {\n        //Good: Iterate through the trigger.new array instead.\n   }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_AvoidHardcodingId",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Hardcoding Ids",
    "description" : "Detects hardcoded Id values in Apex code which can break when changing environments.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nWhen deploying Apex code between sandbox and production environments, or installing Force.com AppExchange packages,\nit is essential to avoid hardcoding IDs in the Apex code. By doing so, if the record IDs change between environments,\nthe logic can dynamically identify the proper data to operate against and not fail.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    void foo() {\n        //Error - hardcoded the record type id\n        if (a.RecordTypeId == '012500000009WAr') {\n            //do some logic here.....\n        } else if (a.RecordTypeId == '0123000000095Km') {\n            //do some logic here for a different record type...\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_AvoidNonExistentAnnotations",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Non-Existent Annotations",
    "description" : "Detect and avoid the use of annotations that do not exist in Apex, which will cause compilation failures in the future.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.5.0\n\nApex supported non existent annotations for legacy reasons.\n            In the future, use of such non-existent annotations could result in broken apex code that will not compile.\n            This will prevent users of garbage annotations from being able to use legitimate annotations added to Apex in the future.\n            A full list of supported annotations can be found at https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.apexcode.meta/apexcode/apex_classes_annotation.htm\n\nExample(s):\n```\n@NonExistentAnnotation public class ClassWithNonexistentAnnotation {\n    @NonExistentAnnotation public void methodWithNonExistentAnnotation() {\n        // ...\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_EmptyCatchBlock",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty Catch Block",
    "description" : "Detects empty catch blocks where exceptions are caught but no action is taken.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nEmpty Catch Block finds instances where an exception is caught, but nothing is done.\nIn most circumstances, this swallows an exception which should either be acted on\nor reported.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void doSomething() {\n    ...\n    try {\n        insert accounts;\n    } catch (DmlException dmle) {\n        // not good\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allowCommentedBlocks",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Empty blocks containing comments will be skipped"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowExceptionNameRegex",
        "default" : "^(ignored|expected)$",
        "description" : "Empty blocks catching exceptions with names matching this regular expression will be skipped"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_EmptyIfStmt",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty If Statements",
    "description" : "Detects and prevents the use of empty 'if' statements where a condition is checked but no action is taken.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nEmpty If Statement finds instances where a condition is checked but nothing is done about it.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar(Integer x) {\n        if (x == 0) {\n            // empty!\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_EmptyStatementBlock",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty Statement Blocks",
    "description" : "This rule detects and avoids empty block statements in code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nEmpty block statements serve no purpose and should be removed.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n\n   private Integer _bar;\n\n   public void setBar(Integer bar) {\n        // empty\n   }\n\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "reportEmptyPrivateNoArgConstructor",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "If false, empty private no-arg constructors are not flagged. This supports a common idiom used by singleton pattern implementations, utility classes, etc."
      },
      {
        "name" : "reportEmptyVirtualMethod",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "If false, empty virtual methods are not flagged. This supports abstract base classes with default no-op implementations."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_EmptyTryOrFinallyBlock",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty Try or Finally Blocks",
    "description" : "This rule checks for try or finally blocks that are empty and have no executable statements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nAvoid empty try or finally blocks - what's the point?\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar() {\n        try {\n          // empty !\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar() {\n        try {\n            Integer x=2;\n        } finally {\n            // empty!\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_EmptyWhileStmt",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty While Statements",
    "description" : "Detects and disallows while loops that have empty bodies.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nEmpty While Statement finds all instances where a while statement does nothing.\nIf it is a timing loop, then you should use Thread.sleep() for it; if it is\na while loop that does a lot in the exit expression, rewrite it to make it clearer.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void bar(Integer a, Integer b) {\n  while (a == b) {\n    // empty!\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_InaccessibleAuraEnabledGetter",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Inaccessible AuraEnabled Getter",
    "description" : "AuraEnabled getters must be declared public or global to be accessible from Lightning components.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.36.0\n\nIn the Summer '21 release, a mandatory security update enforces access modifiers on Apex properties in\nLightning component markup. The update prevents access to private or protected Apex getters from Aura\nand Lightning Web Components.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    @AuraEnabled\n    public Integer counter { private get; set; } // Violating - Private getter is inaccessible to Lightning components\n\n    @AuraEnabled\n    public static Foo bar()\n    {\n        Foo foo = new Foo();\n        foo.counter = 2; \n        return foo;\n    }\n}\n\n        \n\npublic class Foo {\n    @AuraEnabled\n    public Integer counter { protected get; set; } // Violating - Protected getter is inaccessible to Lightning components\n\n    @AuraEnabled\n    public static Foo bar()\n    {\n        Foo foo = new Foo();\n        foo.counter = 2; \n        return foo;\n    }\n}\n\n        \n\npublic class Foo {\n    @AuraEnabled\n    public Integer counter { get; set; } // Compliant - Public getter is accessible to Lightning components\n\n    @AuraEnabled\n    public static Foo bar()\n    {\n        Foo foo = new Foo();\n        foo.counter = 2; \n        return foo;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_MethodWithSameNameAsEnclosingClass",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Methods With Same Name As Enclosing Class",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that classes do not have non-constructor methods with the same name as the class.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nNon-constructor methods should not have the same name as the enclosing class.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class MyClass {\n    // this is OK because it is a constructor\n    public MyClass() {}\n    // this is bad because it is a method\n    public void MyClass() {}\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_OverrideBothEqualsAndHashcode",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Overriding Both Equals And HashCode",
    "description" : "Ensure that classes override both equals() and hashCode() methods together.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.31.0\n\nOverride both `public Boolean equals(Object obj)`, and `public Integer hashCode()`, or override neither.\nEven if you are inheriting a hashCode() from a parent class, consider implementing hashCode and explicitly\ndelegating to your superclass.\n\nThis is especially important when [Using Custom Types in Map Keys and Sets](https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.apexcode.meta/apexcode/langCon_apex_collections_maps_keys_userdefined.htm).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Bar {        // poor, missing a hashCode() method\n    public Boolean equals(Object o) {\n      // do some comparison\n    }\n}\npublic class Baz {        // poor, missing an equals() method\n    public Integer hashCode() {\n      // return some hash value\n    }\n}\npublic class Foo {        // perfect, both methods provided\n    public Boolean equals(Object other) {\n      // do some comparison\n    }\n    public Integer hashCode() {\n      // return some hash value\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_errorprone_TestMethodsMustBeInTestClasses",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Test Methods To Be In Test Classes",
    "description" : "Ensures that test methods are declared inside classes annotated as test classes.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.22.0\n\nTest methods marked as a testMethod or annotated with @IsTest,\n          but not residing in a test class should be moved to a proper\n          class or have the @IsTest annotation added to the class.\n\n          Support for tests inside functional classes was removed in Spring-13 (API Version 27.0),\n          making classes that violate this rule fail compile-time. This rule is mostly usable when\n          dealing with legacy code.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Violating\nprivate class TestClass {\n  @IsTest static void myTest() {\n    // Code here\n  }\n}\n\nprivate class TestClass {\n  static testMethod void myTest() {\n    // Code here\n  }\n}\n\n// Compliant\n@IsTest\nprivate class TestClass {\n  @IsTest static void myTest() {\n    // Code here\n  }\n}\n\n@IsTest\nprivate class TestClass {\n  static testMethod void myTest() {\n    // Code here\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_performance_AvoidDebugStatements",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Debug Statements",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages the use of debug statements in Apex code as they negatively impact performance.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.36.0\n\nDebug statements contribute to longer transactions and consume Apex CPU time even when debug logs are not being captured.\n\nWhen possible make use of other debugging techniques such as the Apex Replay Debugger and Checkpoints that could cover *most* use cases.\n\nFor other valid use cases that the statement is in fact valid make use of the `@SuppressWarnings` annotation or the `//NOPMD` comment.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar() {\n        Account acc = [SELECT Name, Owner.Name FROM Account LIMIT 1];\n        System.debug(accs); // will get reported\n    }\n\n    @SuppressWarnings('PMD.AvoidDebugStatements')\n    public void baz() {\n        try {\n            Account myAccount = bar();\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR, e.getMessage()); // good to go\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_performance_EagerlyLoadedDescribeSObjectResult",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Eager Loading DescribeSObjectResult",
    "description" : "Detects usage of DescribeSObjectResult loaded eagerly with all child relationships, which may cause negative performance impact.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.40.0\n\nThis rule finds `DescribeSObjectResult`s which could have been loaded eagerly via `SObjectType.getDescribe()`.\n\nWhen using `SObjectType.getDescribe()` or `Schema.describeSObjects()` without supplying a `SObjectDescribeOptions`,\nimplicitly it will be using `SObjectDescribeOptions.DEFAULT` and then all\nchild relationships will be loaded eagerly regardless whether this information is needed or not.\nThis has a potential negative performance impact. Instead [`SObjectType.getDescribe(options)`](https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.apexref.meta/apexref/apex_class_Schema_SObjectType.htm#unique_346834793)\nor [`Schema.describeSObjects(SObjectTypes, options)`](https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.apexref.meta/apexref/apex_methods_system_schema.htm#apex_System_Schema_describeSObjects)\nshould be used and a `SObjectDescribeOptions` should be supplied. By using\n`SObjectDescribeOptions.DEFERRED` the describe attributes will be lazily initialized at first use.\n\nLazy loading `DescribeSObjectResult` on picklist fields is not always recommended. The lazy loaded\ndescribe objects might not be 100% accurate. It might be safer to explicitly use\n`SObjectDescribeOptions.FULL` in such a case. The same applies when you need the same `DescribeSObjectResult`\nto be consistent across different contexts and API versions.\n\nProperties:\n\n* `noDefault`: The behavior of `SObjectDescribeOptions.DEFAULT` changes from API Version 43 to 44:\n    With API Version 43, the attributes are loaded eagerly. With API Version 44, they are loaded lazily.\n    Simply using `SObjectDescribeOptions.DEFAULT` doesn't automatically make use of lazy loading.\n    (unless &quot;Use Improved Schema Caching&quot; critical update is applied, `SObjectDescribeOptions.DEFAULT` does fallback\n    to lazy loading)\n    With this property enabled, such usages are found.\n    You might ignore this, if you can make sure, that you don't run a mix of API Versions.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public static void bar(List<Account> accounts) {\n        if (Account.SObjectType.getDescribe(SObjectDescribeOptions.DEFERRED).isCreateable()) {\n            insert accounts;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "noDefault",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Do not allow SObjectDescribeOptions.DEFAULT option to ensure consistent results no matter where getDescribe is called"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_performance_OperationWithLimitsInLoop",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Operations With Limits Inside Loops",
    "description" : "Detects operations inside loops that may cause Salesforce governor limit exceptions, such as DML, SOQL, SOSL, email sending, approval processes, and async scheduling.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.29.0\n\nDatabase class methods, DML operations, SOQL queries, SOSL queries, Approval class methods, Email sending, async scheduling or queueing within loops can cause governor limit exceptions. Instead, try to batch up the data into a list and invoke the operation once on that list of data outside the loop.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Something {\n    public void databaseMethodInsideOfLoop(List<Account> accounts) {\n        for (Account a : accounts) {\n            Database.insert(a);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void dmlInsideOfLoop() {\n        for (Integer i = 0; i < 151; i++) {\n            Account account;\n            // ...\n            insert account;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void soqlInsideOfLoop() {\n        for (Integer i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            List<Account> accounts = [SELECT Id FROM Account];\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void soslInsideOfLoop() {\n        for (Integer i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            List<List<SObject>> searchList = [FIND 'map*' IN ALL FIELDS RETURNING Account (Id, Name), Contact, Opportunity, Lead];\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void messageInsideOfLoop() {\n        for (Integer i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();\n            Messaging.sendEmail(new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage[]{email});\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void approvalInsideOfLoop(Account[] accs) {\n        for (Integer i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            Account acc = accs[i];\n            Approval.ProcessSubmitRequest req = new Approval.ProcessSubmitRequest();\n            req.setObjectId(acc.Id);\n            Approval.process(req);\n            Approval.lock(acc);\n            Approval.unlock(acc);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void asyncInsideOfLoop() {\n        for (Integer i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            System.enqueueJob(new MyQueueable());\n            System.schedule('x', '0 0 0 1 1 ?', new MySchedule());\n            System.scheduleBatch(new MyBatch(), 'x', 1);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexBadCrypto",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "Cryptography",
    "title" : "Avoid Hard-Coded IV and Key in Apex Crypto",
    "description" : "Ensure Apex classes use randomly generated initialization vectors (IVs) and keys for cryptographic operations instead of hard-coded values.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nThe rule makes sure you are using randomly generated IVs and keys for `Crypto` calls.\nHard-wiring these values greatly compromises the security of encrypted data.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    Blob hardCodedIV = Blob.valueOf('Hardcoded IV 123');\n    Blob hardCodedKey = Blob.valueOf('0000000000000000');\n    Blob data = Blob.valueOf('Data to be encrypted');\n    Blob encrypted = Crypto.encrypt('AES128', hardCodedKey, hardCodedIV, data);\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexCRUDViolation",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "Visibility",
    "title" : "Enforce Apex CRUD Permission Checks",
    "description" : "Ensure CRUD permissions are validated before performing SOQL, SOSL, or DML operations in Apex code or enforce user mode to respect field-level security and object permissions.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nThe rule validates you are checking for access permissions before a SOQL/SOSL/DML operation.\nSince Apex runs by default in system mode not having proper permissions checks results in escalation of\nprivilege and may produce runtime errors. This check forces you to handle such scenarios.\n\nSince Winter '23 (API Version 56) you can enforce user mode for database operations by using\n`WITH USER_MODE` in SOQL. This makes Apex to respect Field-level security (FLS) and object\npermissions of the running user. When using user mode, no violation is reported by this rule.\n\nBy default, the rule allows access checks can be performed using system Apex provisions such as\n`DescribeSObjectResult.isAccessible/Createable/etc.`, the SOQL `WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED` clause,\nor using the open source [Force.com ESAPI](https://github.com/forcedotcom/force-dot-com-esapi)\nclass library. Because it is common to use authorization facades to assist with this task, the\nrule also allows configuration of regular expression-based patterns for the methods used to\nauthorize each type of CRUD operation. These pattern are configured via the following properties:\n\n* `createAuthMethodPattern`/`createAuthMethodTypeParamIndex` - a pattern for the method used\n  for create authorization and an optional 0-based index of the parameter passed to that method\n  that denotes the `SObjectType` being authorized for create.\n* `readAuthMethodPattern`/`readAuthMethodTypeParamIndex` - a pattern for the method used\n  for read authorization and an optional 0-based index of the parameter passed to that method\n  that denotes the `SObjectType` being authorized for read.\n* `updateAuthMethodPattern`/`updateAuthMethodTypeParamIndex` - a pattern for the method used\n  for update authorization and an optional 0-based index of the parameter passed to that method\n  that denotes the `SObjectType` being authorized for update.\n* `deleteAuthMethodPattern`/`deleteAuthMethodTypeParamIndex` - a pattern for the method used\n  for delete authorization and an optional 0-based index of the parameter passed to that method\n  that denotes the `SObjectType` being authorized for delete.\n* `undeleteAuthMethodPattern`/`undeleteAuthMethodTypeParamIndex` - a pattern for the method used\n  for undelete authorization and an optional 0-based index of the parameter passed to that method\n  that denotes the `SObjectType` being authorized for undelete.\n* `mergeAuthMethodPattern`/`mergeAuthMethodTypeParamIndex` - a pattern for the method used\n  for merge authorization and an optional 0-based index of the parameter passed to that method\n  that denotes the `SObjectType` being authorized for merge.\n\nThe following example shows how the rule can be configured for the\n[sirono-common](https://github.com/SCWells72/sirono-common)\n[`AuthorizationUtil`](https://github.com/SCWells72/sirono-common#authorization-utilities) class:\n\n```xml\n&lt;rule ref=&quot;category/apex/security.xml/ApexCRUDViolation&quot; message=&quot;Validate CRUD permission before SOQL/DML operation&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;priority&gt;3&lt;/priority&gt;\n    &lt;properties&gt;\n        &lt;property name=&quot;createAuthMethodPattern&quot; value=&quot;AuthorizationUtil\\.(is|assert)(Createable|Upsertable)&quot;/&gt;\n        &lt;property name=&quot;readAuthMethodPattern&quot; value=&quot;AuthorizationUtil\\.(is|assert)Accessible&quot;/&gt;\n        &lt;property name=&quot;updateAuthMethodPattern&quot; value=&quot;AuthorizationUtil\\.(is|assert)(Updateable|Upsertable)&quot;/&gt;\n        &lt;property name=&quot;deleteAuthMethodPattern&quot; value=&quot;AuthorizationUtil\\.(is|assert)Deletable&quot;/&gt;\n        &lt;property name=&quot;undeleteAuthMethodPattern&quot; value=&quot;AuthorizationUtil\\.(is|assert)Undeletable&quot;/&gt;\n        &lt;property name=&quot;mergeAuthMethodPattern&quot; value=&quot;AuthorizationUtil\\.(is|assert)Mergeable&quot;/&gt;\n    &lt;/properties&gt;\n&lt;/rule&gt;\n```\n\nNote: This rule will produce false positives for VF getter methods. In VF getters the access permission\ncheck happens automatically and is not needed explicitly. However, the rule can't reliably determine\nwhether a getter is a VF getter or not and reports a violation in any case. In such cases, the violation\nshould be [suppressed](pmd_userdocs_suppressing_warnings.html).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public Contact foo(String status, String ID) {\n\n        // validate you can actually query what you intend to retrieve\n        Contact c = [SELECT Status__c FROM Contact WHERE Id=:ID WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED];\n\n        // Make sure we can update the database before even trying\n        if (!Schema.sObjectType.Contact.fields.Status__c.isUpdateable()) {\n            return null;\n        }\n\n        c.Status__c = status;\n        update c;\n        return c;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexDangerousMethods",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "InsecureModulesLibraries",
    "title" : "Avoid Calling Apex Dangerous Methods",
    "description" : "Detects calls to potentially dangerous methods that can compromise security, such as disabling CRUD security or logging sensitive data.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nChecks against calling dangerous methods.\n\nFor the time being, it reports:\n\n* Against `FinancialForce`'s `Configuration.disableTriggerCRUDSecurity()`. Disabling CRUD security\nopens the door to several attacks and requires manual validation, which is unreliable.\n* Calling `System.debug` passing sensitive data as parameter, which could lead to exposure\nof private data.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public Foo() {\n        Configuration.disableTriggerCRUDSecurity();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexInsecureEndpoint",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "HTTP",
    "title" : "Avoid Using Insecure HTTP Endpoints in Apex Callouts",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that Apex callouts use secure encrypted communication channels by disallowing HTTP endpoints and requiring HTTPS endpoints.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nChecks against accessing endpoints under plain **http**. You should always use\n**https** for security.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    void foo() {\n        HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();\n        req.setEndpoint('http://localhost:com');\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexOpenRedirect",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "Routes",
    "title" : "Prevent Apex Open Redirect Vulnerabilities",
    "description" : "Ensures that Apex classes redirect only to known, safe locations to avoid open redirect vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nChecks against redirects to user-controlled locations. This prevents attackers from\nredirecting users to phishing sites.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    String unsafeLocation = ApexPage.getCurrentPage().getParameters.get('url_param');\n    PageReference page() {\n       return new PageReference(unsafeLocation);\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexSharingViolations",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "Visibility",
    "title" : "Enforce Declaration of Sharing Model in Apex Classes",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that Apex classes declare an explicit sharing model when performing DML or SOQL/SOSL operations.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nDetect classes declared without explicit sharing mode if DML methods are used. This\nforces the developer to take access restrictions into account before modifying objects.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    // DML operation here\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexSOQLInjection",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "SQLInjection",
    "title" : "Avoid Apex SOQL Injection",
    "description" : "Detects and prevents the usage of untrusted or unescaped variables in Apex SOQL queries to avoid SOQL injection vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nDetects the usage of untrusted / unescaped variables in DML queries.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void test1(String t1) {\n        Database.query('SELECT Id FROM Account' + t1);\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexSuggestUsingNamedCred",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "InsecureStorage",
    "title" : "Suggest Using Named Credentials for Authentication",
    "description" : "Detects hardcoded credentials used in requests to an endpoint and suggests using named credentials instead.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nDetects hardcoded credentials used in requests to an endpoint.\n\nYou should refrain from hardcoding credentials:\n  * They are hard to mantain by being mixed in application code\n  * Particularly hard to update them when used from different classes\n  * Granting a developer access to the codebase means granting knowledge\n     of credentials, keeping a two-level access is not possible.\n  * Using different credentials for different environments is troublesome\n     and error-prone.\n\nInstead, you should use *Named Credentials* and a callout endpoint.\n\nFor more information, you can check [this](https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.apexcode.meta/apexcode/apex_callouts_named_credentials.htm)\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void foo(String username, String password) {\n        Blob headerValue = Blob.valueOf(username + ':' + password);\n        String authorizationHeader = 'BASIC ' + EncodingUtil.base64Encode(headerValue);\n        req.setHeader('Authorization', authorizationHeader);\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexXSSFromEscapeFalse",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Enforce Escaping of Strings in Apex addError Messages",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that strings passed to the addError method in Apex classes are escaped to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nReports on calls to `addError` with disabled escaping. The message passed to `addError`\nwill be displayed directly to the user in the UI, making it prime ground for XSS\nattacks if unescaped.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    Trigger.new[0].addError(vulnerableHTMLGoesHere, false);\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexXSSFromURLParam",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Escape or Sanitize Strings Obtained from URL Parameters in Apex",
    "description" : "Ensure that all string values obtained from URL parameters in Apex classes are properly escaped or sanitized to prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nMakes sure that all values obtained from URL parameters are properly escaped / sanitized\nto avoid XSS attacks.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    String unescapedstring = ApexPage.getCurrentPage().getParameters.get('url_param');\n    String usedLater = unescapedstring;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_AvoidWithStatement",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid With Statement",
    "description" : "This rule detects usage of the 'with' statement in JavaScript, which can lead to ambiguous and unpredictable code behavior.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0.1\n\nAvoid using with - it's bad news\n\nExample(s):\n```\nwith (object) {\n    property = 3; // Might be on object, might be on window: who knows.\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_ConsistentReturn",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Return",
    "description" : "Ensures that functions do not mix return statements with and without a return value.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nECMAScript does provide for return types on functions, and therefore there is no solid rule as to their usage.\nHowever, when a function does use returns they should all have a value, or all with no value.  Mixed return\nusage is likely a bug, or at best poor style.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nfunction foo() {\n    if (condition1) {\n        return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\n// Bad\nfunction bar() {\n    if (condition1) {\n        return;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
