  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_GlobalVariable",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Undeclared Global Variables",
    "description" : "This rule detects and prevents the use of undeclared variables that implicitly become global in JavaScript.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nThis rule helps to avoid using accidently global variables by simply missing the &quot;var&quot; declaration.\nGlobal variables can lead to side-effects that are hard to debug.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nfunction(arg) {\n    notDeclaredVariable = 1;    // this will create a global variable and trigger the rule\n\n    var someVar = 1;            // this is a local variable, that's ok\n\n    window.otherGlobal = 2;     // this will not trigger the rule, although it is a global variable.\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_ScopeForInVariable",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Explicit 'var' Declaration in For-In Loop Variable",
    "description" : "Ensures that the variable used in a for-in loop is explicitly declared with 'var' to avoid unintended variable scope pollution.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nA for-in loop in which the variable name is not explicitly scoped to the enclosing scope with the 'var' keyword can\nrefer to a variable in an enclosing scope outside the nearest enclosing scope.  This will overwrite the\nexisting value of the variable in the outer scope when the body of the for-in is evaluated.  When the for-in loop\nhas finished, the variable will contain the last value used in the for-in, and the original value from before\nthe for-in loop will be gone.  Since the for-in variable name is most likely intended to be a temporary name, it\nis better to explicitly scope the variable name to the nearest enclosing scope with 'var'.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nfunction foo() {\n    var p = 'clean';\n    function() {\n        var obj = { dirty: 'dirty' };\n        for (var p in obj) { // Use 'var' here.\n            obj[p] = obj[p];\n        }\n        return x;\n    }();\n\n    // 'p' still has value of 'clean'.\n}\n// Bad\nfunction bar() {\n    var p = 'clean';\n    function() {\n        var obj = { dirty: 'dirty' };\n        for (p in obj) { // Oh no, missing 'var' here!\n            obj[p] = obj[p];\n        }\n        return x;\n    }();\n\n    // 'p' is trashed and has value of 'dirty'!\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_UseBaseWithParseInt",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Providing Base with parseInt",
    "description" : "Ensures that a radix (base) parameter is always provided when using the parseInt() function.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0.1\n\nThis rule checks for usages of parseInt. While the second parameter is optional and usually defaults\nto 10 (base/radix is 10 for a decimal number), different implementations may behave differently.\nIt also improves readability, if the base is given.\n\nSee also: [parseInt()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/parseInt)\n\nExample(s):\n```\nparseInt(\"010\");    // unclear, could be interpreted as 10 or 7 (with a base of 7)\n\nparseInt(\"10\", 10); // good\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_AssignmentInOperand",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Assignment In Operand",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages assignments within operands, such as in conditional expressions.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid assignments in operands; this can make code more complicated and harder to read.  This is sometime\nindicative of the bug where the assignment operator '=' was used instead of the equality operator '=='.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nvar x = 2;\n// Bad\nif ((x = getX()) == 3) {\n    alert('3!');\n}\n\nfunction getX() {\n    return 3;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allowFor",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the conditional expression of a for statement"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowIf",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the conditional expression of an if statement"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowIncrementDecrement",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow increment or decrement operators within the conditional expression of an if, for, or while statement"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowTernary",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the conditional expression of a ternary operator"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowTernaryResults",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the result expressions of a ternary operator"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowWhile",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the conditional expression of a while statement"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_ForLoopsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce For Loops Use Curly Braces",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that all 'for' loops use curly braces to enclose their body statements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid using 'for' statements without using curly braces.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nfor (var i = 0; i < 42; i++) {\n    foo();\n}\n\n// Bad\nfor (var i = 0; i < 42; i++)\n    foo();\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_IfElseStmtsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Curly Braces for If-Else Statements",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of curly braces for all 'if...else' statements to improve code clarity and prevent errors.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid using if..else statements without using curly braces.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nif (foo) {\n    x++;\n} else {\n    y++;\n}\n\n// Bad\nif (foo)\n    x++;\nelse\n    y++;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_IfStmtsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Braces in If Statements",
    "description" : "Ensures that all if statements use curly braces to enclose their code blocks.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid using if statements without using curly braces.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nif (foo) {\n    x++;\n}\n\n// Bad\nif (foo)\n    x++;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
