      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_LocalVariableCouldBeFinal",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Local Variable Could Be Final",
    "description" : "Detects local variables that are assigned only once and suggests declaring them as final.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 2.2\n\nA local variable assigned only once can be declared final.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Bar {\n    public void foo () {\n    String txtA = \"a\";          // if txtA will not be assigned again it is better to do this:\n    final String txtB = \"b\";\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_LocalVariableNamingConventions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Local Variable Naming Conventions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces configurable naming conventions for local variables, including final variables and catch-clause parameters, ensuring they match specified regular expressions.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.6.0\n\nConfigurable naming conventions for local variable declarations and other locally-scoped\n            variables. This rule reports variable declarations which do not match the regex that applies to their\n            specific kind (e.g. final variable, or catch-clause parameter). Each regex can be configured on the PMD configuration file.\nCheck the [PMD documentation](https://pmd.github.io/pmd-6.55.0/pmd_rules_java_codestyle.html#localvariablenamingconventions) for more information.\n\n            By default this rule uses the standard Java naming convention (Camel case).\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Foo {\n                void bar() {\n                    int localVariable = 1; // This is in camel case, so it's ok\n                    int local_variable = 1; // This will be reported unless you change the regex\n\n                    final int i_var = 1; // final local variables can be configured separately\n\n                    try {\n                        foo();\n                    } catch (IllegalArgumentException e_illegal) {\n                        // exception block parameters can be configured separately\n                    }\n\n                }\n            }\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_LongVariable",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessively Long Variable Names",
    "description" : "This rule checks for variable names that are excessively long, including fields, formal arguments, and local variables, which can make the code harder to read and follow.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 0.3\n\nFields, formal arguments, or local variable names that are too long can make the code difficult to follow.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Something {\n    int reallyLongIntName = -3;             // VIOLATION - Field\n    public static void main( String argumentsList[] ) { // VIOLATION - Formal\n        int otherReallyLongName = -5;       // VIOLATION - Local\n        for (int interestingIntIndex = 0;   // VIOLATION - For\n             interestingIntIndex < 10;\n             interestingIntIndex ++ ) {\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "minimum",
        "default" : "17",
        "description" : "The variable length reporting threshold"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_MDBAndSessionBeanNamingConvention",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce MDB And Session Bean Naming Convention",
    "description" : "Ensures that classes implementing SessionBean or MessageDrivenBean interfaces have names suffixed with 'Bean'.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.0\n\nThe EJB Specification states that any MessageDrivenBean or SessionBean should be suffixed by 'Bean'.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class SomeBean implements SessionBean{}                  // proper name\n\npublic class MissingTheProperSuffix implements SessionBean {}   // non-standard name\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_MethodArgumentCouldBeFinal",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Method Arguments to be Declared Final When Not Reassigned",
    "description" : "This rule checks if method parameters that are never reassigned within the method are declared final.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 2.2\n\nA method argument that is never re-assigned within the method can be declared final.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void foo1 (String param) {       // do stuff with param never assigning it\n\n}\n\npublic void foo2 (final String param) { // better, do stuff with param never assigning it\n\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_MethodNamingConventions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Method Naming Conventions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces configurable naming conventions for method declarations to ensure they match specified regex patterns.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.2\n\nConfigurable naming conventions for method declarations. This rule reports\n            method declarations which do not match the regex that applies to their\n            specific kind (e.g. JUnit test or native method). Each regex can be configured on the PMD configuration file.\nCheck the [PMD documentation](https://pmd.github.io/pmd-6.55.0/pmd_rules_java_codestyle.html#methodnamingconventions) for more information.\n\n            By default this rule uses the standard Java naming convention (Camel case).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void fooStuff() {\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "junit3TestPattern",
        "default" : "test[A-Z0-9][a-zA-Z0-9]*",
        "description" : "junit3TestPattern: Regex which applies to JUnit 3 test method names"
      },
      {
        "name" : "junit4TestPattern",
        "default" : "[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*",
        "description" : "junit4TestPattern: Regex which applies to JUnit 4 test method names"
      },
      {
        "name" : "junit5TestPattern",
        "default" : "[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*",
        "description" : "junit5TestPattern: Regex which applies to JUnit 5 test method names"
      },
      {
        "name" : "methodPattern",
        "default" : "[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*",
        "description" : "methodPattern: Regex which applies to instance method names"
      },
      {
        "name" : "nativePattern",
        "default" : "[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*",
        "description" : "nativePattern: Regex which applies to native method names"
      },
      {
        "name" : "staticPattern",
        "default" : "[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*",
        "description" : "staticPattern: Regex which applies to static method names"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_NoPackage",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Package Declaration",
    "description" : "All classes, interfaces, enums, and annotations must belong to a named package, disallowing usage of the default package.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.3\n\nDetects when a class, interface, enum or annotation does not have a package definition.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// no package declaration\npublic class ClassInDefaultPackage {\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_OnlyOneReturn",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Only One Return Statement",
    "description" : "Ensures that a method has only one exit point, which should be the last statement in the method.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.0\n\nA method should have only one exit point, and that should be the last statement in the method.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class OneReturnOnly1 {\n  public String foo(int x) {\n    if (x > 0) {\n      return \"hey\";   // first exit\n    }\n    return \"hi\";    // second exit\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_PackageCase",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Lowercase Package Names",
    "description" : "Package names should not contain uppercase characters.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.3\n\nDetects when a package definition contains uppercase characters.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npackage com.MyCompany;  // should be lowercase name\n\npublic class SomeClass {\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_PrematureDeclaration",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Premature Variable Declaration",
    "description" : "This rule checks for variables that are declared before they are actually needed, especially before code blocks that might exit early without using the variable.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nChecks for variables that are defined before they might be used. A reference is deemed to be premature if it is created right before a block of code that doesn't use it that also has the ability to return or throw an exception.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic int getLength(String[] strings) {\n\n    int length = 0; // declared prematurely\n\n    if (strings == null || strings.length == 0) return 0;\n\n    for (String str : strings) {\n        length += str.length();\n    }\n\n    return length;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_RemoteInterfaceNamingConvention",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Suffixes in Remote Interface Names of Session EJBs",
    "description" : "Remote interfaces of Session EJBs should not have suffixes such as 'Session', 'EJB', or 'Bean'.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.0\n\nRemote Interface of a Session EJB should not have a suffix.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n/* Poor Session suffix */\npublic interface BadSuffixSession extends javax.ejb.EJBObject {}\n\n/* Poor EJB suffix */\npublic interface BadSuffixEJB extends javax.ejb.EJBObject {}\n\n/* Poor Bean suffix */\npublic interface BadSuffixBean extends javax.ejb.EJBObject {}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_RemoteSessionInterfaceNamingConvention",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Remote Session Interface Naming Convention",
    "description" : "Ensures that Remote Home interfaces of Session EJBs are named with the suffix 'Home'.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.0\n\nA Remote Home interface type of a Session EJB should be suffixed by 'Home'.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic interface MyBeautifulHome extends javax.ejb.EJBHome {}       // proper name\n\npublic interface MissingProperSuffix extends javax.ejb.EJBHome {}   // non-standard name\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_ShortClassName",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Short Class Names",
    "description" : "This rule discourages the use of short class names, typically those with fewer than five characters.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nShort Classnames with fewer than e.g. five characters are not recommended.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "minimum",
        "default" : "5",
        "description" : "Number of characters that are required as a minimum for a class name."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_ShortMethodName",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Short Method Names",
    "description" : "This rule discourages the use of very short method names that are not descriptive.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 0.3\n\nMethod names that are very short are not helpful to the reader.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class ShortMethod {\n    public void a( int i ) { // Violation\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "minimum",
        "default" : "3",
        "description" : "Number of characters that are required as a minimum for a method name."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_ShortVariable",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Short Variable Names",
    "description" : "This rule flags variables with very short names that are not helpful for code readability, except for loop counters in for and for-each loops.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 0.3\n\nFields, local variables, or parameter names that are very short are not helpful to the reader.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Something {\n    private int q = 15;                         // field - too short\n    public static void main( String as[] ) {    // formal arg - too short\n        int r = 20 + q;                         // local var - too short\n        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {          // not a violation (inside 'for' loop)\n            r += q;\n        }\n        for (Integer i : numbers) {             // not a violation (inside 'for-each' loop)\n            r += q;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "minimum",
        "default" : "3",
        "description" : "Number of characters that are required as a minimum for a variable name."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_TooManyStaticImports",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Too Many Static Imports",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages the overuse of static imports in code to prevent readability and maintainability issues.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.1\n\nIf you overuse the static import feature, it can make your program unreadable and\nunmaintainable, polluting its namespace with all the static members you import.\nReaders of your code (including you, a few months after you wrote it) will not know\nwhich class a static member comes from (Sun 1.5 Language Guide).\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport static Lennon;\nimport static Ringo;\nimport static George;\nimport static Paul;\nimport static Yoko; // Too much !\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "maximumStaticImports",
        "default" : "4",
        "description" : "All static imports can be disallowed by setting this to 0"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessaryAnnotationValueElement",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary 'value' Element in Annotations",
    "description" : "Avoid using the 'value' element in annotations when it is the only element present.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.2.0\n\nAvoid the use of value in annotations when it's the only element.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n@TestClassAnnotation(value = \"TEST\")\npublic class Foo {\n\n    @TestMemberAnnotation(value = \"TEST\")\n    private String y;\n\n    @TestMethodAnnotation(value = \"TEST\")\n    public void bar() {\n        int x = 42;\n        return;\n    }\n}\n\n// should be\n\n@TestClassAnnotation(\"TEST\")\npublic class Foo {\n\n    @TestMemberAnnotation(\"TEST\")\n    private String y;\n\n    @TestMethodAnnotation(\"TEST\")\n    public void bar() {\n        int x = 42;\n        return;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessaryCast",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Casts",
    "description" : "This rule detects and avoids unnecessary type casts, especially when accessing collection elements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.24.0\n\nThis rule detects when a cast is unnecessary while accessing collection elements. This rule is mostly useful\nfor old java code before generics where introduced with java 1.5.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class UnnecessaryCastSample {\n    public void method() {\n        List<String> stringList = Arrays.asList(\"a\", \"b\");\n        String element = (String) stringList.get(0); // this cast is unnecessary\n        String element2 = stringList.get(0);\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessaryConstructor",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Constructor",
    "description" : "Detects and avoids constructors that are unnecessary because the compiler automatically generates them.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.0\n\nThis rule detects when a constructor is not necessary; i.e., when there is only one constructor and the\nconstructor is identical to the default constructor. The default constructor should has same access\nmodifier as the declaring class. In an enum type, the default constructor is implicitly private.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n  public Foo() {}\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessaryFullyQualifiedName",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Fully Qualified Names",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages the redundant use of fully qualified class names when an import statement already allows using the simple class name.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nImport statements allow the use of non-fully qualified names.  The use of a fully qualified name\nwhich is covered by an import statement is redundant.  Consider using the non-fully qualified name.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Foo {\n    private java.util.List list1;   // Unnecessary FQN\n    private List list2;             // More appropriate given import of 'java.util.List'\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessaryImport",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Import Statements",
    "description" : "Detects and reports import statements that are unnecessary because they are unused, duplicated, or implicitly in scope.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.34.0\n\nReports import statements that can be removed. They are either unused,\n            duplicated, or the members they import are already implicitly in scope,\n            because they're in java.lang, or the current package.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport java.io.File;            // not used, can be removed\n            import java.util.Collections;   // used below\n            import java.util.*;             // so this one is not used\n\n            import java.lang.Object;        // imports from java.lang, unnecessary\n            import java.lang.Object;        // duplicate, unnecessary\n\n            public class Foo {\n                static Object emptyList() {\n                    return Collections.emptyList();\n                }\n            }\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessaryLocalBeforeReturn",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Local Variable Before Return",
    "description" : "This rule checks for unnecessary local variables that are assigned a value and immediately returned, suggesting to return the value directly instead.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.3\n\nAvoid the creation of unnecessary local variables\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n   public int foo() {\n     int x = doSomething();\n     return x;  // instead, just 'return doSomething();'\n   }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessaryModifier",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Modifiers",
    "description" : "Detects and flags modifiers in Java code that are redundant because they are implied by the context, such as public, static, final, or abstract in interfaces, annotations, nested classes, and enums.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.02\n\nFields in interfaces and annotations are automatically `public static final`, and methods are `public abstract`.\nClasses, interfaces or annotations nested in an interface or annotation are automatically `public static`\n(all nested interfaces and annotations are automatically static).\nNested enums are automatically `static`.\nFor historical reasons, modifiers which are implied by the context are accepted by the compiler, but are superfluous.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic @interface Annotation {\n    public abstract void bar();     // both abstract and public are ignored by the compiler\n    public static final int X = 0;  // public, static, and final all ignored\n    public static class Bar {}      // public, static ignored\n    public static interface Baz {}  // ditto\n}\npublic interface Foo {\n    public abstract void bar();     // both abstract and public are ignored by the compiler\n    public static final int X = 0;  // public, static, and final all ignored\n    public static class Bar {}      // public, static ignored\n    public static interface Baz {}  // ditto\n}\npublic class Bar {\n    public static interface Baz {}  // static ignored\n    public static enum FoorBar {    // static ignored\n        FOO;\n    }\n}\npublic class FooClass {\n    static record BarRecord() {}     // static ignored\n}\npublic interface FooInterface {\n    static record BarRecord() {}     // static ignored\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessaryReturn",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Return Statements",
    "description" : "This rule detects and discourages the use of return statements that are unnecessary, such as a return at the end of a void method.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.3\n\nAvoid the use of unnecessary return statements.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar() {\n        int x = 42;\n        return;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UnnecessarySemicolon",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Semicolons",
    "description" : "Detects and reports unnecessary semicolons (empty statements) in Java code that can be removed without affecting program behavior.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.46.0\n\nReports unnecessary semicolons (so called &quot;empty statements&quot; and &quot;empty declarations&quot;).\n            These can be removed without changing the program. The Java grammar\n            allows them for historical reasons, but they should be avoided.\n            \n            This rule will not report empty statements that are syntactically \n            required, for instance, because they are the body of a control statement.\n\n            This rule replaces EmptyStatementNotInLoop.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Foo {\n    {\n        toString();; // one of these semicolons is unnecessary\n        if (true); // this semicolon is not unnecessary, but it could be an empty block instead (not reported)\n    }\n}; // this semicolon is unnecessary\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UseDiamondOperator",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Use of Diamond Operator",
    "description" : "This rule enforces replacing explicit type arguments in constructor calls with the diamond operator to enable type inference and reduce code duplication.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.11.0\n\nUse the diamond operator to let the type be inferred automatically. With the Diamond operator it is possible\nto avoid duplication of the type parameters.\nInstead, the compiler is now able to infer the parameter types for constructor calls,\nwhich makes the code also more readable.\n\nThe diamond operator has been introduced with java 7. However, type inference has been improved further\nwith java8, rendering more type parameters unnecessary. This is only possible with java8 and the resulting\ncode won't compile with java7. If you use java7, make sure to enable `java7Compatibility` for this rule to avoid\nfalse positives.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nList<String> strings = new ArrayList<String>(); // unnecessary duplication of type parameters\nList<String> stringsWithDiamond = new ArrayList<>(); // using the diamond operator is more concise\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "java7Compatibility",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "If disabled, the rule shows also violations that are applicable for java8+"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UselessParentheses",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Useless Parentheses",
    "description" : "Detects and flags unnecessary parentheses in code that do not affect the logic or precedence.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nUseless parentheses should be removed.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n\n    private int _bar1;\n    private Integer _bar2;\n\n    public void setBar(int n) {\n        _bar1 = Integer.valueOf((n)); // here\n        _bar2 = (n); // and here\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UselessQualifiedThis",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Useless Qualified This",
    "description" : "Detects and reports unnecessary usage of qualified 'this' within the same class where unqualified 'this' can be used directly.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.4.0\n\nReports qualified this usages in the same class.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    final Foo otherFoo = Foo.this;  // use \"this\" directly\n\n    public void doSomething() {\n         final Foo anotherFoo = Foo.this;  // use \"this\" directly\n    }\n\n    private ActionListener returnListener() {\n        return new ActionListener() {\n            @Override\n            public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {\n                doSomethingWithQualifiedThis(Foo.this);  // This is fine\n            }\n        };\n    }\n\n    private class Foo3 {\n        final Foo myFoo = Foo.this;  // This is fine\n    }\n\n    private class Foo2 {\n        final Foo2 myFoo2 = Foo2.this;  // Use \"this\" direclty\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UseShortArrayInitializer",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use Short Array Initializer",
    "description" : "Enforces using the short form of array initialization without explicitly using 'new'.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.15.0\n\nWhen declaring and initializing array fields or variables, it is not necessary to explicitly create a new array\nusing `new`. Instead one can simply define the initial content of the array as a expression in curly braces.\n\nE.g. `int[] x = new int[] { 1, 2, 3 };` can be written as `int[] x = { 1, 2, 3 };`.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nFoo[] x = new Foo[] { ... }; // Overly verbose\nFoo[] x = { ... }; //Equivalent to above line\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_codestyle_UseUnderscoresInNumericLiterals",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Use of Underscores in Numeric Literals",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that numeric literals above a certain length use underscores to separate every third digit for improved readability.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.10.0\n\nSince Java 1.7, numeric literals can use underscores to separate digits. This rule enforces that\n            numeric literals above a certain length use these underscores to increase readability.\n\n            The rule only supports decimal (base 10) literals for now. The acceptable length under which literals\n            are not required to have underscores is configurable via a property. Even under that length, underscores\n            that are misplaced (not making groups of 3 digits) are reported.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    private int num = 1000000; // should be 1_000_000\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "acceptableDecimalLength",
        "default" : "4",
        "description" : "Length under which literals in base 10 are not required to have underscores"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_AbstractClassWithoutAnyMethod",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Abstract Classes Without Any Method",
    "description" : "Detects abstract classes that do not declare any abstract methods, which are likely used solely to prevent instantiation.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2\n\nIf an abstract class does not provide any methods, it may be acting as a simple data container\nthat is not meant to be instantiated. In this case, it is probably better to use a private or\nprotected constructor in order to prevent instantiation than make the class misleadingly abstract.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic abstract class Example {\n    String field;\n    int otherField;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_AvoidCatchingGenericException",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Catching Generic Exception",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding the practice of catching generic exceptions such as Exception, RuntimeException, or NullPointerException in try-catch blocks.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2.6\n\nAvoid catching generic exceptions such as NullPointerException, RuntimeException, Exception in try-catch block.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npackage com.igate.primitive;\n\npublic class PrimitiveType {\n\n    public void downCastPrimitiveType() {\n        try {\n            System.out.println(\" i [\" + i + \"]\");\n        } catch(Exception e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        } catch(RuntimeException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        } catch(NullPointerException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_AvoidDeeplyNestedIfStmts",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Deeply Nested If Statements",
    "description" : "This rule flags deeply nested if-then statements that reduce code readability and maintainability.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.0\n\nAvoid creating deeply nested if-then statements since they are harder to read and error-prone to maintain.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n  public void bar(int x, int y, int z) {\n    if (x>y) {\n      if (y>z) {\n        if (z==x) {\n         // !! too deep\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_AvoidRethrowingException",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Rethrowing Exception",
    "description" : "Detects catch blocks that catch an exception only to rethrow the same exception without any additional handling.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.8\n\nCatch blocks that merely rethrow a caught exception only add to code size and runtime complexity.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void bar() {\n    try {\n        // do something\n    }  catch (SomeException se) {\n       throw se;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_AvoidThrowingNewInstanceOfSameException",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Throwing New Instance Of Same Exception",
    "description" : "Detects catch blocks that catch an exception only to wrap and rethrow it as a new instance of the same exception type.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2.5\n\nCatch blocks that merely rethrow a caught exception wrapped inside a new instance of the same type only add to\ncode size and runtime complexity.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void bar() {\n    try {\n        // do something\n    } catch (SomeException se) {\n        // harmless comment\n        throw new SomeException(se);\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_AvoidThrowingNullPointerException",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Throwing NullPointerException Manually",
    "description" : "This rule discourages manually throwing NullPointerException in code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.8\n\nAvoid throwing NullPointerExceptions manually. These are confusing because most people will assume that the\nvirtual machine threw it.  To avoid a method being called with a null parameter, you may consider\nusing an IllegalArgumentException instead, making it clearly seen as a programmer-initiated exception.\nHowever, there are better ways to handle this:\n\n&gt;*Effective Java, 3rd Edition, Item 72: Favor the use of standard exceptions*\n&gt;\n&gt;Arguably, every erroneous method invocation boils down to an illegal argument or state,\nbut other exceptions are standardly used for certain kinds of illegal arguments and states.\nIf a caller passes null in some parameter for which null values are prohibited, convention dictates that\nNullPointerException be thrown rather than IllegalArgumentException.\n\nTo implement that, you are encouraged to use `java.util.Objects.requireNonNull()`\n(introduced in Java 1.7). This method is designed primarily for doing parameter\nvalidation in methods and constructors with multiple parameters.\n\nYour parameter validation could thus look like the following:\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    private String exampleValue;\n\n    void setExampleValue(String exampleValue) {\n      // check, throw and assignment in a single standard call\n      this.exampleValue = Objects.requireNonNull(exampleValue, &quot;exampleValue must not be null!&quot;);\n    }\n  }\n```\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    void bar() {\n        throw new NullPointerException();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_AvoidThrowingRawExceptionTypes",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Throwing Raw Exception Types",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding throwing raw exception types such as Exception, RuntimeException, Throwable, or Error.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.8\n\nAvoid throwing certain exception types. Rather than throw a raw RuntimeException, Throwable,\nException, or Error, use a subclassed exception or error instead.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar() throws Exception {\n        throw new Exception();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_AvoidUncheckedExceptionsInSignatures",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Declaring Unchecked Exceptions in Method Signatures",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that methods or constructors should not explicitly declare unchecked exceptions in their throws clause.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.13.0\n\nA method or constructor should not explicitly declare unchecked exceptions in its\n`throws` clause. Java doesn't force the caller to handle an unchecked exception,\nso it's unnecessary except for documentation. A better practice is to document the\nexceptional cases with a `@throws` Javadoc tag, which allows being more descriptive.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void foo() throws RuntimeException {\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_ClassWithOnlyPrivateConstructorsShouldBeFinal",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Final on Classes With Only Private Constructors",
    "description" : "Ensures that classes which have only private constructors are declared as final to prevent subclassing.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.1\n\nA class with only private constructors should be final, unless the private constructor\nis invoked by an inner class.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {  //Should be final\n    private Foo() { }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_CognitiveComplexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid High Cognitive Complexity",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods with high cognitive complexity, which makes them difficult to read and maintain.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.35.0\n\nMethods that are highly complex are difficult to read and more costly to maintain. If you include too much decisional\n            logic within a single method, you make its behavior hard to understand and more difficult to modify.\n\n            Cognitive complexity is a measure of how difficult it is for humans to read and understand a method. Code that contains\n            a break in the control flow is more complex, whereas the use of language shorthands doesn't increase the level of\n            complexity. Nested control flows can make a method more difficult to understand, with each additional nesting of the\n            control flow leading to an increase in cognitive complexity.\n\n            Information about Cognitive complexity can be found in the original paper here:\n            &lt;https://www.sonarsource.com/docs/CognitiveComplexity.pdf&gt;\n\n            By default, this rule reports methods with a complexity of 15 or more. Reported methods should be broken down into less\n            complex components.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n  // Has a cognitive complexity of 0\n  public void createAccount() {\n    Account account = new Account(\"PMD\");\n    // save account\n  }\n\n  // Has a cognitive complexity of 1\n  public Boolean setPhoneNumberIfNotExisting(Account a, String phone) {\n    if (a.phone == null) {                          // +1\n      a.phone = phone;\n      return true;\n    }\n\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  // Has a cognitive complexity of 4\n  public void updateContacts(List<Contact> contacts) {\n    List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new ArrayList<Contact>();\n\n    for (Contact contact : contacts) {                           // +1\n      if (contact.department.equals(\"Finance\")) {                // +2 (nesting = 1)\n        contact.title = \"Finance Specialist\";\n        contactsToUpdate.add(contact);\n      } else if (contact.department.equals(\"Sales\")) {           // +1\n        contact.title = \"Sales Specialist\";\n        contactsToUpdate.add(contact);\n      }\n    }\n    // save contacts\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_CollapsibleIfStatements",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Collapsible If Statements",
    "description" : "Detects nested if statements that can be combined into a single if statement using boolean operators.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.1\n\nSometimes two consecutive 'if' statements can be consolidated by separating their conditions with a boolean short-circuit operator.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nvoid bar() {\n    if (x) {            // original implementation\n        if (y) {\n            // do stuff\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nvoid bar() {\n    if (x && y) {        // optimized implementation\n        // do stuff\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_CouplingBetweenObjects",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid High Coupling Between Objects",
    "description" : "Detects classes that have a high number of different object types as members, local variables, or return types, indicating high coupling.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.04\n\nThis rule counts the number of unique attributes, local variables, and return types within an object.\nA number higher than the specified threshold can indicate a high degree of coupling.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport com.Blah;\nimport org.Bar;\nimport org.Bardo;\n\npublic class Foo {\n    private Blah var1;\n    private Bar var2;\n\n    //followed by many imports of unique objects\n    ObjectC doWork() {\n        Bardo var55;\n        ObjectA var44;\n        ObjectZ var93;\n        return something();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_CyclomaticComplexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid High Cyclomatic Complexity",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods or classes with cyclomatic complexity exceeding a defined threshold, indicating overly complex and hard to maintain code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.03\n\nThe complexity of methods directly affects maintenance costs and readability. Concentrating too much decisional logic\nin a single method makes its behaviour hard to read and change.\n\nCyclomatic complexity assesses the complexity of a method by counting the number of decision points in a method,\nplus one for the method entry. Decision points are places where the control flow jumps to another place in the\nprogram. As such, they include all control flow statements, such as `if`, `while`, `for`, and `case`. For more\ndetails on the calculation, see the documentation of the [Cyclo metric](pmd_java_metrics_index.html#cyclomatic-complexity-cyclo).\n\nGenerally, numbers ranging from 1-4 denote low complexity, 5-7 denote moderate complexity, 8-10 denote\nhigh complexity, and 11+ is very high complexity. By default, this rule reports methods with a complexity &gt;= 10.\nAdditionally, classes with many methods of moderate complexity get reported as well once the total of their\nmethods' complexities reaches 80, even if none of the methods was directly reported.\n\nReported methods should be broken down into several smaller methods. Reported classes should probably be broken down\ninto subcomponents.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Foo {\n  void baseCyclo() {                // Cyclo = 1\n    highCyclo();\n  }\n\n  void highCyclo() {                // Cyclo = 10: reported!\n    int x = 0, y = 2;\n    boolean a = false, b = true;\n\n    if (a && (y == 1 ? b : true)) { // +3\n      if (y == x) {                 // +1\n        while (true) {              // +1\n          if (x++ < 20) {           // +1\n            break;                  // +1\n          }\n        }\n      } else if (y == t && !d) {    // +2\n        x = a ? y : x;              // +1\n      } else {\n        x = 2;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_DataClass",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Data Class",
    "description" : "Detects classes that are simple data holders exposing most of their state without complex functionality, known as Data Classes.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nData Classes are simple data holders, which reveal most of their state, and\nwithout complex functionality. The lack of functionality may indicate that\ntheir behaviour is defined elsewhere, which is a sign of poor data-behaviour\nproximity. By directly exposing their internals, Data Classes break encapsulation,\nand therefore reduce the system's maintainability and understandability. Moreover,\nclasses tend to strongly rely on their data representation, which makes for a brittle\ndesign.\n\nRefactoring a Data Class should focus on restoring a good data-behaviour proximity. In\nmost cases, that means moving the operations defined on the data back into the class.\nIn some other cases it may make sense to remove entirely the class and move the data\ninto the former client classes.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class DataClass {\n\n  // class exposes public attributes\n  public String name = \"\";\n  public int bar = 0;\n  public int na = 0;\n\n  private int bee = 0;\n\n  // and private ones through getters\n  public void setBee(int n) {\n    bee = n;\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_design_DoNotExtendJavaLangError",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Extending java.lang.Error",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that exceptions should not extend java.lang.Error.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.0\n\nErrors are system exceptions. Do not extend them.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo extends Error { }\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
