  {
    "id" : "category_java_documentation_UncommentedEmptyConstructor",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Document Empty Constructor",
    "description" : "Ensures that empty constructors have explanatory comments to clarify their intentional emptiness.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.4\n\nUncommented Empty Constructor finds instances where a constructor does not\ncontain statements, but there is no comment. By explicitly commenting empty\nconstructors it is easier to distinguish between intentional (commented)\nand unintentional empty constructors.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic Foo() {\n  // This constructor is intentionally empty. Nothing special is needed here.\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignoreExplicitConstructorInvocation",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Ignore explicit constructor invocation when deciding whether constructor is empty or not"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_documentation_UncommentedEmptyMethodBody",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Document Empty Method Body",
    "description" : "Ensures that empty method bodies are explicitly commented to clarify intent.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.4\n\nUncommented Empty Method Body finds instances where a method body does not contain\nstatements, but there is no comment. By explicitly commenting empty method bodies\nit is easier to distinguish between intentional (commented) and unintentional\nempty methods.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void doSomething() {\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
