  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_AvoidFieldNameMatchingMethodName",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Field Name Matching Method Name",
    "description" : "This rule detects when a field has the same name as a method in the same class, which can cause confusion between data and actions.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nIt can be confusing to have a field name with the same name as a method. While this is permitted,\nhaving information (field) and actions (method) is not clear naming. Developers versed in\nSmalltalk often prefer this approach as the methods denote accessor methods.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    Object bar;\n    // bar is data or an action or both?\n    void bar() {\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_AvoidFieldNameMatchingTypeName",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Field Name Matching Type Name",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages fields whose names match the declaring class or interface name, as it can cause confusion.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nIt is somewhat confusing to have a field name matching the declaring type name.\nThis probably means that type and/or field names should be chosen more carefully.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo extends Bar {\n    int foo;    // There is probably a better name that can be used\n}\npublic interface Operation {\n    int OPERATION = 1; // There is probably a better name that can be used\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_AvoidInstanceofChecksInCatchClause",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Instanceof Checks In Catch Clause",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that each exception type is caught in its own catch clause rather than using instanceof checks inside a catch block.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nEach caught exception type should be handled in its own catch clause.\n\nExample(s):\n```\ntry { // Avoid this\n    // do something\n} catch (Exception ee) {\n    if (ee instanceof IOException) {\n        cleanup();\n    }\n}\n\ntry {  // Prefer this:\n    // do something\n} catch (IOException ee) {\n    cleanup();\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_AvoidLiteralsInIfCondition",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Literals In If Condition",
    "description" : "This rule detects the use of hard-coded literals in if conditional statements to improve code maintainability.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2.6\n\nAvoid using hard-coded literals in conditional statements. By declaring them as static variables\nor private members with descriptive names maintainability is enhanced. By default, the literals &quot;-1&quot; and &quot;0&quot; are ignored.\nMore exceptions can be defined with the property &quot;ignoreMagicNumbers&quot;.\n\nThe rule doesn't consider deeper expressions by default, but this can be enabled via the property `ignoreExpressions`.\nWith this property set to false, if-conditions like `i == 1 + 5` are reported as well. Note that in that case,\nthe property ignoreMagicNumbers is not taken into account, if there are multiple literals involved in such an expression.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nprivate static final int MAX_NUMBER_OF_REQUESTS = 10;\n\npublic void checkRequests() {\n\n    if (i == 10) {                        // magic number, buried in a method\n      doSomething();\n    }\n\n    if (i == MAX_NUMBER_OF_REQUESTS) {    // preferred approach\n      doSomething();\n    }\n\n    if (aString.indexOf('.') != -1) {}     // magic number -1, by default ignored\n    if (aString.indexOf('.') >= 0) { }     // alternative approach\n\n    if (aDouble > 0.0) {}                  // magic number 0.0\n    if (aDouble >= Double.MIN_VALUE) {}    // preferred approach\n\n    // with rule property \"ignoreExpressions\" set to \"false\"\n    if (i == pos + 5) {}  // violation: magic number 5 within an (additive) expression\n    if (i == pos + SUFFIX_LENGTH) {} // preferred approach\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignoreExpressions",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "If true, only literals in simple if conditions are considered. Otherwise literals in expressions are checked, too."
      },
      {
        "name" : "ignoreMagicNumbers",
        "default" : "-1,0",
        "description" : "Comma-separated list of magic numbers, that should be ignored"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_AvoidLosingExceptionInformation",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Losing Exception Information",
    "description" : "Detects statements in catch blocks that call exception accessors without using their returned information, leading to redundant code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2.6\n\nStatements in a catch block that invoke accessors on the exception without using the information\nonly add to code size.  Either remove the invocation, or use the return result.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void bar() {\n    try {\n        // do something\n    } catch (SomeException se) {\n        se.getMessage();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_AvoidMultipleUnaryOperators",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Multiple Unary Operators",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages the use of multiple unary operators in expressions as they may be bugs or cause confusion.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2\n\nThe use of multiple unary operators may be problematic, and/or confusing.\nEnsure that the intended usage is not a bug, or consider simplifying the expression.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// These are typo bugs, or at best needlessly complex and confusing:\nint i = - -1;\nint j = + - +1;\nint z = ~~2;\nboolean b = !!true;\nboolean c = !!!true;\n\n// These are better:\nint i = 1;\nint j = -1;\nint z = 2;\nboolean b = true;\nboolean c = false;\n\n// And these just make your brain hurt:\nint i = ~-2;\nint j = -~7;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_AvoidUsingOctalValues",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Octal Values",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that integer literals should not start with zero unless they are intended to be octal values.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.9\n\nInteger literals should not start with zero since this denotes that the rest of literal will be\ninterpreted as an octal value.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nint i = 012;    // set i with 10 not 12\nint j = 010;    // set j with 8 not 10\nk = i * j;      // set k with 80 not 120\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_BrokenNullCheck",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Broken Null Check",
    "description" : "Detects method calls on objects that may be null due to incorrect null check logic.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.8\n\nThe null check is broken since it will throw a NullPointerException itself.\nIt is likely that you used || instead of &amp;&amp; or vice versa.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic String bar(String string) {\n  // should be &&\n    if (string!=null || !string.equals(\"\"))\n        return string;\n  // should be ||\n    if (string==null && string.equals(\"\"))\n        return string;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_CallSuperFirst",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Calling Super First in Method",
    "description" : "Ensures that the super method is called at the start of an overriding method.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2.5\n\nSuper should be called at the start of the method\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class DummyActivity extends Activity {\n    public void onCreate(Bundle bundle) {\n        // missing call to super.onCreate(bundle)\n        foo();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_CallSuperLast",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Calling Super Last in Method",
    "description" : "Ensures that calls to superclass methods (super) occur at the end of overriding methods.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2.5\n\nSuper should be called at the end of the method\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class DummyActivity extends Activity {\n    public void onPause() {\n        foo();\n        // missing call to super.onPause()\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_CheckSkipResult",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Check Skip Result",
    "description" : "Ensure that the value returned by the skip() method of an InputStream is checked to verify the requested number of bytes has been skipped.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nThe skip() method may skip a smaller number of bytes than requested. Check the returned value to find out if it was the case or not.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n\n   private FileInputStream _s = new FileInputStream(\"file\");\n\n   public void skip(int n) throws IOException {\n      _s.skip(n); // You are not sure that exactly n bytes are skipped\n   }\n\n   public void skipExactly(int n) throws IOException {\n      while (n != 0) {\n         long skipped = _s.skip(n);\n         if (skipped == 0)\n            throw new EOFException();\n         n -= skipped;\n      }\n   }\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_ClassCastExceptionWithToArray",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid ClassCastException by Correct Usage of Collection.toArray()",
    "description" : "Detects incorrect usage of Collection.toArray() that leads to ClassCastException when casting the result to a specific array type.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.4\n\nWhen deriving an array of a specific class from your Collection, one should provide an array of\nthe same class as the parameter of the toArray() method. Doing otherwise you will will result\nin a ClassCastException.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCollection c = new ArrayList();\nInteger obj = new Integer(1);\nc.add(obj);\n\n    // this would trigger the rule (and throw a ClassCastException if executed)\nInteger[] a = (Integer [])c.toArray();\n\n   // this is fine and will not trigger the rule\nInteger[] b = (Integer [])c.toArray(new Integer[c.size()]);\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_CloneMethodMustBePublic",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Clone Method to Be Public",
    "description" : "Ensures that the clone() method is declared public if the class implements the Cloneable interface.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.4.0\n\nThe java Manual says &quot;By convention, classes that implement this interface should override\nObject.clone (which is protected) with a public method.&quot;\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo implements Cloneable {\n    @Override\n    protected Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException { // Violation, must be public\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Foo implements Cloneable {\n    @Override\n    protected Foo clone() { // Violation, must be public\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Foo implements Cloneable {\n    @Override\n    public Object clone() // Ok\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_CloneMethodMustImplementCloneable",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Clone Method Only If Implementing Cloneable",
    "description" : "The clone() method should only be implemented by classes that implement the Cloneable interface.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.9\n\nThe method clone() should only be implemented if the class implements the Cloneable interface with the exception of\na final method that only throws CloneNotSupportedException.\n\nThe rule can also detect, if the class implements or extends a Cloneable class.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class MyClass {\n public Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {\n  return foo;\n }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_CloneMethodReturnTypeMustMatchClassName",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Clone Method Return Type Matches Class Name",
    "description" : "Ensures that the clone() method's return type matches the class name when the class implements Cloneable.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.4.0\n\nIf a class implements cloneable the return type of the method clone() must be the class name. That way, the caller\nof the clone method doesn't need to cast the returned clone to the correct type.\n\nNote: This is only possible with Java 1.5 or higher.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo implements Cloneable {\n    @Override\n    protected Object clone() { // Violation, Object must be Foo\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Foo implements Cloneable {\n    @Override\n    public Foo clone() { //Ok\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_CloseResource",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Ensure Resources Are Closed After Use",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that resources such as java.sql.Connection, java.sql.Statement, java.sql.ResultSet, and any subtype of java.lang.AutoCloseable are properly closed after use to prevent resource leaks.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.2.2\n\nEnsure that resources (like `java.sql.Connection`, `java.sql.Statement`, and `java.sql.ResultSet` objects\nand any subtype of `java.lang.AutoCloseable`) are always closed after use.\nFailing to do so might result in resource leaks.\n\nNote: It suffices to configure the super type, e.g. `java.lang.AutoClosable`, so that this rule automatically triggers\non any subtype (e.g. `java.io.FileInputStream`). Additionally specifying `java.sql.Connection` helps in detecting\nthe types, if the type resolution / auxclasspath is not correctly setup.\n\nNote: Since PMD 6.16.0 the default value for the property `types` contains `java.lang.AutoCloseable` and detects\nnow cases where the standard `java.io.*Stream` classes are involved. In order to restore the old behaviour,\njust remove &quot;AutoCloseable&quot; from the types.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Bar {\n    public void withSQL() {\n        Connection c = pool.getConnection();\n        try {\n            // do stuff\n        } catch (SQLException ex) {\n           // handle exception\n        } finally {\n            // oops, should close the connection using 'close'!\n            // c.close();\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void withFile() {\n        InputStream file = new FileInputStream(new File(\"/tmp/foo\"));\n        try {\n            int c = file.in();\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            // handle exception\n        } finally {\n            // TODO: close file\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_CompareObjectsWithEquals",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Object Comparison Using equals()",
    "description" : "Ensure that object references are compared using equals() method instead of '==' operator.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.2\n\nUse `equals()` to compare object references; avoid comparing them with `==`.\n\nSince comparing objects with named constants is useful in some cases (eg, when\ndefining constants for sentinel values), the rule ignores comparisons against\nfields with all-caps name (eg `this == SENTINEL`), which is a common naming\nconvention for constant fields.\n\nYou may allow some types to be compared by reference by listing the exceptions\nin the `typesThatCompareByReference` property.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Foo {\n  boolean bar(String a, String b) {\n    return a == b;\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "typesThatCompareByReference",
        "default" : "java.lang.Enum,java.lang.Class",
        "description" : "List of canonical type names for which reference comparison is allowed."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_ComparisonWithNaN",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Comparison With NaN",
    "description" : "Detects comparisons with NaN (Not-a-Number) values that always return false due to IEEE floating-point rules.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.36.0\n\nReports comparisons with double and float `NaN` (Not-a-Number) values.\n            These are [specified](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se8/html/jls-15.html#jls-15.21.1)\n            to have unintuitive behavior: NaN is considered unequal to itself.\n            This means a check like `someDouble == Double.NaN` will always return\n            false, even if `someDouble` is really the NaN value. To test whether a\n            value is the NaN value, one should instead use `Double.isNaN(someDouble)`\n            (or `Float.isNaN`). The `!=` operator should be treated similarly.\n            Finally, comparisons like `someDouble &lt;= Double.NaN` are nonsensical\n            and will always evaluate to false.\n            \n            This rule has been renamed from &quot;BadComparison&quot; in PMD 6.36.0.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nboolean x = (y == Double.NaN);\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_ConstructorCallsOverridableMethod",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Calling Overridable Methods in Constructors",
    "description" : "Detects calls to overridable methods during object construction, which can lead to unexpected behavior and bugs.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.04\n\nCalling overridable methods during construction poses a risk of invoking methods on an incompletely\nconstructed object and can be difficult to debug.\nIt may leave the sub-class unable to construct its superclass or forced to replicate the construction\nprocess completely within itself, losing the ability to call super().  If the default constructor\ncontains a call to an overridable method, the subclass may be completely uninstantiable.   Note that\nthis includes method calls throughout the control flow graph - i.e., if a constructor Foo() calls a\nprivate method bar() that calls a public method buz(), this denotes a problem.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class SeniorClass {\n  public SeniorClass(){\n      toString(); //may throw NullPointerException if overridden\n  }\n  public String toString(){\n    return \"IAmSeniorClass\";\n  }\n}\npublic class JuniorClass extends SeniorClass {\n  private String name;\n  public JuniorClass(){\n    super(); //Automatic call leads to NullPointerException\n    name = \"JuniorClass\";\n  }\n  public String toString(){\n    return name.toUpperCase();\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_DetachedTestCase",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Detect Detached JUnit Test Case",
    "description" : "Identifies JUnit test methods that are not properly annotated and may be detached from the test framework.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.13.0\n\nThe method appears to be a test case since it has public or default visibility,\nnon-static access, no arguments, no return value, has no annotations, but is a\nmember of a class that has one or more JUnit test cases. If it is a utility\nmethod, it should likely have private visibility. If it is an ignored test, it\nshould be annotated with @Test and @Ignore.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class MyTest {\n    @Test\n    public void someTest() {\n    }\n\n    // violation: Not annotated\n    public void someOtherTest () {\n    }\n\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_DoNotCallGarbageCollectionExplicitly",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Explicit Garbage Collection Calls",
    "description" : "This rule prohibits explicit calls to garbage collection methods such as System.gc() and Runtime.getRuntime().gc().",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2\n\nCalls to System.gc(), Runtime.getRuntime().gc(), and System.runFinalization() are not advised. Code should have the\nsame behavior whether the garbage collection is disabled using the option -Xdisableexplicitgc or not.\nMoreover, &quot;modern&quot; jvms do a very good job handling garbage collections. If memory usage issues unrelated to memory\nleaks develop within an application, it should be dealt with JVM options rather than within the code itself.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class GCCall {\n    public GCCall() {\n        // Explicit gc call !\n        System.gc();\n    }\n\n    public void doSomething() {\n        // Explicit gc call !\n        Runtime.getRuntime().gc();\n    }\n\n    public explicitGCcall() {\n        // Explicit gc call !\n        System.gc();\n    }\n\n    public void doSomething() {\n        // Explicit gc call !\n        Runtime.getRuntime().gc();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_DoNotExtendJavaLangThrowable",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Extending java.lang.Throwable",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that exceptions should not extend java.lang.Throwable directly.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.0.0\n\nExtend Exception or RuntimeException instead of Throwable.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo extends Throwable { }\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_DoNotHardCodeSDCard",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Hardcoding SD Card Path",
    "description" : "This rule detects hardcoded usage of the /sdcard path in Android applications and encourages using the appropriate API to access external storage.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2.6\n\nUse Environment.getExternalStorageDirectory() instead of &quot;/sdcard&quot;\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class MyActivity extends Activity {\n    protected void foo() {\n        String storageLocation = \"/sdcard/mypackage\";   // hard-coded, poor approach\n\n       storageLocation = Environment.getExternalStorageDirectory() + \"/mypackage\"; // preferred approach\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_DoNotTerminateVM",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Terminating the JVM in J2EE/JEE Applications",
    "description" : "This rule prevents the use of System.exit() and equivalent calls that terminate the JVM in J2EE/JEE web applications.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.1\n\nWeb applications should not call `System.exit()`, since only the web container or the\napplication server should stop the JVM. Otherwise a web application would terminate all other applications\nrunning on the same application server.\n\nThis rule also checks for the equivalent calls `Runtime.getRuntime().exit()` and `Runtime.getRuntime().halt()`.\n\nThis rule has been renamed from &quot;DoNotCallSystemExit&quot; in PMD 6.29.0.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void bar() {\n    System.exit(0);                 // never call this when running in an application server!\n}\npublic void foo() {\n    Runtime.getRuntime().exit(0);   // never stop the JVM manually, the container will do this.\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_DoNotThrowExceptionInFinally",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Throwing Exception In Finally Block",
    "description" : "This rule detects throw statements inside finally blocks, which can obscure control flow and mask other exceptions.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2\n\nThrowing exceptions within a 'finally' block is confusing since they may mask other exceptions\nor code defects.\nNote: This is a PMD implementation of the Lint4j rule &quot;A throw in a finally block&quot;\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar() {\n        try {\n            // Here do some stuff\n        } catch( Exception e) {\n            // Handling the issue\n        } finally {\n            // is this really a good idea ?\n            throw new Exception();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_DontImportSun",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Importing sun.* Packages",
    "description" : "This rule checks for imports from the 'sun.*' packages in Java code and flags them as they are non-portable and subject to change.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.5\n\nAvoid importing anything from the 'sun.*' packages.  These packages are not portable and are likely to change.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport sun.misc.foo;\npublic class Foo {}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_DontUseFloatTypeForLoopIndices",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Float Type For Loop Indices",
    "description" : "This rule disallows using floating point float type variables as loop indices in for loops.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.3\n\nDon't use floating point for loop indices. If you must use floating point, use double\nunless you're certain that float provides enough precision and you have a compelling\nperformance need (space or time).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Count {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    final int START = 2000000000;\n    int count = 0;\n    for (float f = START; f < START + 50; f++)\n      count++;\n      //Prints 0 because (float) START == (float) (START + 50).\n      System.out.println(count);\n      //The termination test misbehaves due to floating point granularity.\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_EmptyCatchBlock",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty Catch Blocks",
    "description" : "Detects catch blocks that are empty, which silently swallow exceptions without handling or reporting them.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 0.1\n\nEmpty Catch Block finds instances where an exception is caught, but nothing is done.\nIn most circumstances, this swallows an exception which should either be acted on\nor reported.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void doSomething() {\n    try {\n        FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(\"/tmp/bugger\");\n    } catch (IOException ioe) {\n        // not good\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allowCommentedBlocks",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Empty blocks containing comments will be skipped"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowExceptionNameRegex",
        "default" : "^(ignored|expected)$",
        "description" : "Empty blocks catching exceptions with names matching this regular expression will be skipped"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_EmptyFinalizer",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty Finalize Methods",
    "description" : "Detects and disallows empty finalize methods in Java classes.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.5\n\nEmpty finalize methods serve no purpose and should be removed. Note that Oracle has declared Object.finalize() as deprecated since JDK 9.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n   protected void finalize() {}\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_EqualsNull",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using equals() to Compare Against Null",
    "description" : "This rule detects the use of the equals() method to compare an object against null, which is incorrect and should be replaced with the '==' operator.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.9\n\nTests for null should not use the equals() method. The '==' operator should be used instead.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nString x = \"foo\";\n\nif (x.equals(null)) {   // bad form\n    doSomething();\n}\n\nif (x == null) {        // preferred\n    doSomething();\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_FinalizeDoesNotCallSuperFinalize",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Finalize Method Calls Super Finalize",
    "description" : "Ensures that a finalize() method implementation calls super.finalize() as its last statement.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.5\n\nIf the finalize() is implemented, its last action should be to call super.finalize. Note that Oracle has declared Object.finalize() as deprecated since JDK 9.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nprotected void finalize() {\n    something();\n    // neglected to call super.finalize()\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_FinalizeOnlyCallsSuperFinalize",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Finalize Method That Only Calls Super Finalize",
    "description" : "Checks that the finalize() method does more than just call super.finalize().",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.5\n\nIf the finalize() is implemented, it should do something besides just calling super.finalize(). Note that Oracle has declared Object.finalize() as deprecated since JDK 9.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nprotected void finalize() {\n    super.finalize();\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_FinalizeOverloaded",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Overloading finalize Method",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that methods named finalize() are not overloaded with parameters, as such methods will not be called by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.5\n\nMethods named finalize() should not have parameters.  It is confusing and most likely an attempt to\noverload Object.finalize(). It will not be called by the VM.\n\nNote that Oracle has declared Object.finalize() as deprecated since JDK 9.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    // this is confusing and probably a bug\n    protected void finalize(int a) {\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_FinalizeShouldBeProtected",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Protected Access Modifier on finalize() Method",
    "description" : "Ensures that when the finalize() method is overridden, it is declared with protected access to prevent inappropriate invocation by other classes.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.1\n\nWhen overriding the finalize(), the new method should be set as protected.  If made public,\nother classes may invoke it at inappropriate times.\n\nNote that Oracle has declared Object.finalize() as deprecated since JDK 9.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void finalize() {\n    // do something\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_IdempotentOperations",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Idempotent Operations",
    "description" : "This rule detects idempotent operations, such as assigning a variable to itself, which have no effect and are redundant.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 2.0\n\nAvoid idempotent operations - they have no effect.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n public void bar() {\n  int x = 2;\n  x = x;\n }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_ImplicitSwitchFallThrough",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Implicit Switch Fall Through",
    "description" : "Detects switch statements where case blocks do not end with break or return, leading to implicit fall-through behavior.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nSwitch statements without break or return statements for each case option\nmay indicate problematic behaviour. Empty cases are ignored as these indicate an intentional fall-through.\n\nThis rule has been renamed from &quot;MissingBreakInSwitch&quot; in PMD 6.37.0.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void bar(int status) {\n    switch(status) {\n      case CANCELLED:\n        doCancelled();\n        // break; hm, should this be commented out?\n      case NEW:\n        doNew();\n        // is this really a fall-through?\n      case REMOVED:\n        doRemoved();\n        // what happens if you add another case after this one?\n      case OTHER: // empty case - this is interpreted as an intentional fall-through\n      case ERROR:\n        doErrorHandling();\n        break;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_InstantiationToGetClass",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Instantiation Just to Get Class",
    "description" : "This rule detects and prevents creating an object instance solely to call getClass() on it, recommending the use of the .class member instead.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 2.0\n\nAvoid instantiating an object just to call getClass() on it; use the .class public member instead.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// replace this\nClass c = new String().getClass();\n\n// with this:\nClass c = String.class;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_InvalidLogMessageFormat",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Invalid Log Message Format",
    "description" : "Detects logging calls where the number of message placeholders does not match the number of arguments provided.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nCheck for messages in slf4j and log4j2 (since 6.19.0) loggers with non matching number of arguments and placeholders.\n\nSince 6.32.0 in addition to parameterized message placeholders (`{}`) also format specifiers of string formatted\nmessages are supported (`%s`).\n\nThis rule has been renamed from &quot;InvalidSlf4jMessageFormat&quot; in PMD 6.19.0.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nLOGGER.error(\"forget the arg {}\");\nLOGGER.error(\"forget the arg %s\");\nLOGGER.error(\"too many args {}\", \"arg1\", \"arg2\");\nLOGGER.error(\"param {}\", \"arg1\", new IllegalStateException(\"arg\")); //The exception is shown separately, so is correct.\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_JumbledIncrementer",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Modifying Outer Loop Incrementer in Inner Loop",
    "description" : "Detects and prevents modification of an outer loop's increment variable within an inner loop's update expression.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.0\n\nAvoid jumbled loop incrementers - its usually a mistake, and is confusing even if intentional.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class JumbledIncrementerRule1 {\n    public void foo() {\n        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {          // only references 'i'\n            for (int k = 0; k < 20; i++) {      // references both 'i' and 'k'\n                System.out.println(\"Hello\");\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_JUnitSpelling",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Misspelling JUnit setUp and tearDown Methods",
    "description" : "Detects misspelled setUp or tearDown methods in JUnit 3 test classes.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.0\n\nIn JUnit 3, the setUp method is used to set up all data entities required in running tests.\n            The tearDown method is used to clean up all data entities required in running tests.\n            You should not misspell method name if you want your test to set up and clean up everything correctly.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport junit.framework.*;\n\npublic class Foo extends TestCase {\n    public void setup() {}    // oops, should be setUp\n    public void TearDown() {} // oops, should be tearDown\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_JUnitStaticSuite",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Public Static Modifier on JUnit suite() Method",
    "description" : "Checks that the suite() method in a JUnit test class is declared both public and static so that JUnit can correctly invoke it to obtain the TestSuite.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.0\n\nThe suite() method in a JUnit test needs to be both public and static.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport junit.framework.*;\n\npublic class Foo extends TestCase {\n    public void suite() {}         // oops, should be static\n    private static void suite() {} // oops, should be public\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_MethodWithSameNameAsEnclosingClass",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Methods With Same Name As Enclosing Class",
    "description" : "This rule detects non-constructor methods that have the same name as their enclosing class, which is not allowed in Java.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.5\n\nNon-constructor methods should not have the same name as the enclosing class.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class MyClass {\n\n    public MyClass() {}         // this is OK because it is a constructor\n\n    public void MyClass() {}    // this is bad because it is a method\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_MisplacedNullCheck",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Misplaced Null Check",
    "description" : "This rule detects misplaced null checks that can lead to NullPointerException if the variable is null when accessed before the null check.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.5\n\nThe null check here is misplaced. If the variable is null a NullPointerException will be thrown.\nEither the check is useless (the variable will never be &quot;null&quot;) or it is incorrect.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    void bar() {\n        if (a.equals(baz) && a != null) {} // a could be null, misplaced null check\n\n        if (a != null && a.equals(baz)) {} // correct null check\n    }\n}\n\n        \n\npublic class Foo {\n    void bar() {\n        if (a.equals(baz) || a == null) {} // a could be null, misplaced null check\n\n        if (a == null || a.equals(baz)) {} // correct null check\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_MissingSerialVersionUID",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce SerialVersionUID in Serializable Classes",
    "description" : "Classes implementing Serializable should define a serialVersionUID field.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nSerializable classes should provide a serialVersionUID field.\nThe serialVersionUID field is also needed for abstract base classes. Each individual class in the inheritance\nchain needs an own serialVersionUID field. See also [Should an abstract class have a serialVersionUID](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/893259/should-an-abstract-class-have-a-serialversionuid).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo implements java.io.Serializable {\n    String name;\n    // Define serialization id to avoid serialization related bugs\n    // i.e., public static final long serialVersionUID = 4328743;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_MissingStaticMethodInNonInstantiatableClass",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Missing Static Method In Non Instantiatable Class",
    "description" : "Detects classes that cannot be instantiated and do not provide any static methods or fields, making them unusable.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nA class that has private constructors and does not have any static methods or fields cannot be used.\n\nWhen one of the private constructors is annotated with one of the annotations, then the class is not considered\nnon-instantiatable anymore and no violation will be reported.\nSee the property `annotations`.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// This class is unusable, since it cannot be\n// instantiated (private constructor),\n// and no static method can be called.\n\npublic class Foo {\n  private Foo() {}\n  void foo() {}\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "annotations",
        "default" : "org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired,javax.inject.Inject",
        "description" : "If a constructor is annotated with one of these annotations, then the class is ignored."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_MoreThanOneLogger",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid More Than One Logger in a Class",
    "description" : "Detects classes that contain more than one logger instance.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 2.0\n\nNormally only one logger is used in each class. This rule supports slf4j, log4j, Java Util Logging and\nlog4j2 (since 6.19.0).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    Logger log = Logger.getLogger(Foo.class.getName());\n    // It is very rare to see two loggers on a class, normally\n    // log information is multiplexed by levels\n    Logger log2= Logger.getLogger(Foo.class.getName());\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_NonCaseLabelInSwitchStatement",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Non-Case Labels in Switch Statements",
    "description" : "Detects the presence of non-case labels (e.g., named break/continue labels) within switch statements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.5\n\nA non-case label (e.g. a named break/continue label) was present in a switch statement.\nThis legal, but confusing. It is easy to mix up the case labels and the non-case labels.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n  void bar(int a) {\n   switch (a) {\n     case 1:\n       // do something\n       break;\n     mylabel: // this is legal, but confusing!\n       break;\n     default:\n       break;\n    }\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_NonSerializableClass",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Non-Serializable Fields in Serializable Classes",
    "description" : "Detects fields in a Serializable class that are not themselves serializable, which can cause serialization failures.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.1\n\nIf a class is marked as `Serializable`, then all fields need to be serializable as well. In order to exclude\na field, it can be marked as transient. Static fields are not considered.\n\nThis rule reports all fields, that are not serializable.\n\nIf a class implements the methods to perform manual serialization (`writeObject`, `readObject`) or uses\na replacement object (`writeReplace`, `readResolve`) then this class is ignored.\n\nNote: This rule has been revamped with PMD 6.52.0. It was previously called &quot;BeanMembersShouldSerialize&quot;.\nThe property `prefix` has been deprecated, since in a serializable class all fields have to be\nserializable regardless of the name.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Buzz implements java.io.Serializable {\n    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;\n\n    private transient int someFoo;          // good, it's transient\n    private static int otherFoo;            // also OK, it's static\n    private java.io.FileInputStream stream; // bad - FileInputStream is not serializable\n\n    public void setStream(FileInputStream stream) {\n        this.stream = stream;\n    }\n\n    public int getSomeFoo() {\n          return this.someFoo;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_NonStaticInitializer",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Non-Static Initializer Blocks",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages the use of non-static initializer blocks in Java classes.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.5\n\nA non-static initializer block will be called any time a constructor is invoked (just prior to\ninvoking the constructor).  While this is a valid language construct, it is rarely used and is\nconfusing.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class MyClass {\n  // this block gets run before any call to a constructor\n  {\n    System.out.println(\"I am about to construct myself\");\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_NullAssignment",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Null Assignment",
    "description" : "Detects assignments of null to variables outside of their declaration, which is considered a code smell.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.02\n\nAssigning a &quot;null&quot; to a variable (outside of its declaration) is usually bad form.  Sometimes, this type\nof assignment is an indication that the programmer doesn't completely understand what is going on in the code.\n\nNOTE: This sort of assignment may used in some cases to dereference objects and encourage garbage collection.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic void bar() {\n  Object x = null; // this is OK\n  x = new Object();\n     // big, complex piece of code here\n  x = null; // this is not required\n     // big, complex piece of code here\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_OverrideBothEqualsAndHashcode",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Overriding Both equals() and hashCode()",
    "description" : "Ensure that classes override both equals() and hashCode() methods together to maintain consistent behavior.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 0.4\n\nOverride both public boolean Object.equals(Object other), and public int Object.hashCode(), or override neither.  Even if you are inheriting a hashCode() from a parent class, consider implementing hashCode and explicitly delegating to your superclass.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Bar {        // poor, missing a hashcode() method\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n      // do some comparison\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Baz {        // poor, missing an equals() method\n    public int hashCode() {\n      // return some hash value\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Foo {        // perfect, both methods provided\n    public boolean equals(Object other) {\n      // do some comparison\n    }\n    public int hashCode() {\n      // return some hash value\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_ProperCloneImplementation",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Proper Clone Implementation",
    "description" : "Ensures that the clone() method is implemented using super.clone() to correctly clone objects.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.4\n\nObject clone() should be implemented with super.clone().\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Foo{\n    public Object clone(){\n        return new Foo(); // This is bad\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_ProperLogger",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Proper Logger Declaration",
    "description" : "Ensures that loggers are declared as private static final and associated with the correct class.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.3\n\nA logger should normally be defined private static final and be associated with the correct class.\n`private final Log log;` is also allowed for rare cases where loggers need to be passed around,\nwith the restriction that the logger needs to be passed into the constructor.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n\n    private static final Log LOG = LogFactory.getLog(Foo.class);    // proper way\n\n    protected Log LOG = LogFactory.getLog(Testclass.class);         // wrong approach\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "loggerClass",
        "default" : "Log",
        "description" : "Class name of the logger"
      },
      {
        "name" : "loggerName",
        "default" : "log",
        "description" : "Name of the Logger instance variable"
      },
      {
        "name" : "staticLoggerName",
        "default" : "LOG",
        "description" : "Name of the static Logger variable"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_ReturnEmptyCollectionRatherThanNull",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Return Empty Collection Rather Than Null",
    "description" : "This rule enforces returning an empty collection instead of null from methods that return collections or arrays.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.37.0\n\nFor any method that returns an collection (such as an array, Collection or Map), it is better to return\nan empty one rather than a null reference. This removes the need for null checking all results and avoids\ninadvertent NullPointerExceptions.\n\nSee Effective Java, 3rd Edition, Item 54: Return empty collections or arrays instead of null\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Example {\n    // Not a good idea...\n    public int[] badBehavior() {\n        // ...\n        return null;\n    }\n\n    // Good behavior\n    public String[] bonnePratique() {\n        //...\n        return new String[0];\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_ReturnFromFinallyBlock",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Returning From Finally Block",
    "description" : "This rule detects and prevents returning a value from a finally block in Java code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.05\n\nAvoid returning from a finally block, this can discard exceptions.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Bar {\n    public String foo() {\n        try {\n            throw new Exception( \"My Exception\" );\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            throw e;\n        } finally {\n            return \"A. O. K.\"; // return not recommended here\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_errorprone_SimpleDateFormatNeedsLocale",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Specifying Locale in SimpleDateFormat Instantiation",
    "description" : "Ensure that a Locale is specified when creating instances of SimpleDateFormat to guarantee locale-appropriate date formatting.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 2.0\n\nBe sure to specify a Locale when creating SimpleDateFormat instances to ensure that locale-appropriate\nformatting is used.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n  // Should specify Locale.US (or whatever)\n  private SimpleDateFormat sdf = new SimpleDateFormat(\"pattern\");\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
