  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_TooFewBranchesForASwitchStatement",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Switch Statements with Too Few Branches",
    "description" : "This rule detects switch statements with less than three branches and suggests using if-else statements instead for better readability and efficiency.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2\n\nSwitch statements are intended to be used to support complex branching behaviour. Using a switch for only a few\ncases is ill-advised, since switches are not as easy to understand as if-else statements. In these cases use the\nif-else statement to increase code readability.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// With a minimumNumberCaseForASwitch of 3\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar() {\n        switch (condition) {\n            case ONE:\n                instruction;\n                break;\n            default:\n                break; // not enough for a 'switch' stmt, a simple 'if' stmt would have been more appropriate\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "minimumNumberCaseForASwitch",
        "default" : "3",
        "description" : "Minimum number of branches for a switch"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseArrayListInsteadOfVector",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use ArrayList Instead Of Vector",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of ArrayList instead of Vector when thread-safe operation is not required.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nArrayList is a much better Collection implementation than Vector if thread-safe operation is not required.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport java.util.*;\npublic class SimpleTest extends TestCase {\n    public void testX() {\n    Collection c1 = new Vector();\n    Collection c2 = new ArrayList();    // achieves the same with much better performance\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseArraysAsList",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use Arrays.asList Instead of Manual Loop to Create List",
    "description" : "This rule encourages using java.util.Arrays.asList() to create a List from an array instead of manually copying elements in a loop.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.5\n\nThe `java.util.Arrays` class has a `asList()` method that should be used when you want to create a new List from\nan array of objects. It is faster than executing a loop to copy all the elements of the array one by one.\n\nNote that the result of `Arrays.asList()` is backed by the specified array,\nchanges in the returned list will result in the array to be modified.\nFor that reason, it is not possible to add new elements to the returned list of `Arrays.asList()`\n(UnsupportedOperationException).\nYou must use `new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(Arrays.asList(...))` if that is inconvenient for you (e.g. because of concurrent access).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Test {\n    public void foo(Integer[] ints) {\n        // could just use Arrays.asList(ints)\n        List<Integer> l = new ArrayList<>(100);\n        for (int i = 0; i < ints.length; i++) {\n            l.add(ints[i]);\n        }\n\n        List<Integer> anotherList = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < ints.length; i++) {\n            anotherList.add(ints[i].toString()); // won't trigger the rule\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseIndexOfChar",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use indexOf(char) Instead of indexOf(String) for Single Characters",
    "description" : "Enforces using String.indexOf(char) rather than String.indexOf(String) when searching for a single character to improve performance.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.5\n\nUse String.indexOf(char) when checking for the index of a single character; it executes faster.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nString s = \"hello world\";\n// avoid this\nif (s.indexOf(\"d\") {}\n// instead do this\nif (s.indexOf('d') {}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseIOStreamsWithApacheCommonsFileItem",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Memory Intensive FileItem get() or getString()",
    "description" : "This rule discourages using FileItem.get() or FileItem.getString() methods which load the entire file into memory, potentially causing memory exhaustion.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.25.0\n\nProblem: Use of [FileItem.get()](https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-fileupload/apidocs/org/apache/commons/fileupload/FileItem.html#get--)\nand [FileItem.getString()](https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-fileupload/apidocs/org/apache/commons/fileupload/FileItem.html#getString--)\ncould exhaust memory since they load the entire file into memory.\n\nSolution: Use [FileItem.getInputStream()](https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-fileupload/apidocs/org/apache/commons/fileupload/FileItem.html#getInputStream--)\nand buffering.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport org.apache.commons.fileupload.FileItem;\n\npublic class FileStuff {\n   private String bad(FileItem fileItem) {\n        return fileItem.getString();\n   }\n\n   private InputStream good(FileItem fileItem) {\n        return fileItem.getInputStream();\n   }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UselessStringValueOf",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Useless String.valueOf Calls",
    "description" : "Detects unnecessary use of String.valueOf when appending to strings, where the argument can be appended directly.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.8\n\nNo need to call String.valueOf to append to a string; just use the valueOf() argument directly.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic String convert(int i) {\n    String s;\n    s = \"a\" + String.valueOf(i);    // not required\n    s = \"a\" + i;                    // preferred approach\n    return s;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseStringBufferForStringAppends",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use StringBuilder or StringBuffer for String Appends",
    "description" : "This rule enforces using StringBuilder or StringBuffer instead of '+=' operator for string concatenation in loops or multiple appends.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.1\n\nThe use of the '+=' operator for appending strings causes the JVM to create and use an internal StringBuffer.\nIf a non-trivial number of these concatenations are being used then the explicit use of a StringBuilder or\nthreadsafe StringBuffer is recommended to avoid this.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    String inefficientConcatenation() {\n        String result = \"\";\n        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            // warning: this concatenation will create one new StringBuilder per iteration\n            result += getStringFromSomeWhere(i);\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    String efficientConcatenation() {\n        // better would be to use one StringBuilder for the entire loop\n        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            result.append(getStringFromSomeWhere(i));\n        }\n        return result.toString();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseStringBufferLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use StringBuffer Length Instead of toString",
    "description" : "Detects inefficient usage of StringBuffer.toString() to check length or emptiness, recommending use of StringBuffer.length() instead.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.4\n\nUse StringBuffer.length() to determine StringBuffer length rather than using StringBuffer.toString().equals(&quot;&quot;)\nor StringBuffer.toString().length() == ...\n\nExample(s):\n```\nStringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer();\n\nif (sb.toString().equals(\"\")) {}        // inefficient\n\nif (sb.length() == 0) {}                // preferred\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_security_HardCodedCryptoKey",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "Cryptography",
    "title" : "Avoid Hard Coded Crypto Keys",
    "description" : "This rule detects the use of hard coded encryption keys in the source code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.4.0\n\nDo not use hard coded values for cryptographic operations. Please store keys outside of source code.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    void good() {\n        SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec = new SecretKeySpec(Properties.getKey(), \"AES\");\n    }\n\n    void bad() {\n        SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec = new SecretKeySpec(\"my secret here\".getBytes(), \"AES\");\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
