  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessivePackageSpecificationLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Package Specification Length",
    "description" : "This rule flags package specifications that are excessively long in PL/SQL code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nExcessive class file lengths are usually indications that the class may be burdened with excessive\nresponsibilities that could be provided by external classes or functions. In breaking these methods\napart the code becomes more managable and ripe for reuse.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPACKAGE Foo AS\n    PROCEDURE bar1;\n    PROCEDURE bar2;\n    PROCEDURE bar3;\n\n    ...\n\n    PROCEDURE barN;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessiveParameterList",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Parameter List",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods or procedures with too many parameters, which can make code harder to maintain and understand.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nMethods with numerous parameters are a challenge to maintain, especially if most of them share the\nsame datatype. These situations usually denote the need for new objects to wrap the numerous parameters.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPROCEDURE addPerson(        -- too many arguments liable to be mixed up\n    birthYear pls_integer, birthMonth pls_integer, birthDate pls_integer, height pls_integer, weight pls_integer, ssn pls_integer) {\n\n    . . . .\nEND ADDPERSON;\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPROCEDURE addPerson(        -- preferred approach\n    birthdate DATE, measurements BodyMeasurements , ssn INTEGER) BEGIN\n\n    . . . .\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessiveTypeLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Type Length",
    "description" : "This rule detects object type specifications in PL/SQL that are excessively long, indicating that the type may have too many responsibilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nExcessive class file lengths are usually indications that the class may be burdened with excessive\nresponsibilities that could be provided by external classes or functions. In breaking these methods\napart the code becomes more managable and ripe for reuse.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nTYPE BODY Foo AS\n    MEMBER PROCEDURE bar1 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar1;\n    MEMBER PROCEDURE bar2 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar2;\n    MEMBER PROCEDURE bar3 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar3;\n\n\n    MEMBER PROCEDURE barN IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END barn;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_NcssMethodCount",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Maximum NCSS Method Count",
    "description" : "This rule checks the Non-Commenting Source Statements (NCSS) line count of methods and flags methods that exceed a certain NCSS threshold, indicating they are too large or complex.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nThis rule uses the NCSS (Non-Commenting Source Statements) algorithm to determine the number of lines\nof code for a given method. NCSS ignores comments, and counts actual statements. Using this algorithm,\nlines of code that are split are counted as one.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY AS\n FUNCTION methd RETURN INTEGER IS\n BEGIN\n   RETURN 1;;\n END;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_NcssObjectCount",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce NCSS Object Count Limit",
    "description" : "This rule checks the Non-Commenting Source Statements (NCSS) line count for Oracle database objects to ensure they do not exceed a specified threshold.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nThis rule uses the NCSS (Non-Commenting Source Statements) algorithm to determine the number of lines\nof code for a given Oracle object. NCSS ignores comments, and counts actual statements. Using this algorithm,\nlines of code that are split are counted as one.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE pkg_\n PROCEDURE Foo IS\n BEGIN\n --this class only has 6 NCSS lines\n     super();\n     super();\n END;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_NPathComplexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid High NPath Complexity",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods with high NPath complexity, which is the number of acyclic execution paths through the method.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nThe NPath complexity of a method is the number of acyclic execution paths through that method.\nA threshold of 200 is generally considered the point where measures should be taken to reduce\ncomplexity and increase readability.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPROCEDURE bar AS BEGIN  -- this is something more complex than it needs to be,\n    if (y) THEN -- it should be broken down into smaller methods or functions\n        for j IN 0 .. j-1 LOOP\n            if (j > r) THEN\n                doSomething;\n                while (f < 5 ) LOOP\n                    anotherThing;\n                    f := f - 27;\n                    END LOOP;\n            else\n                tryThis();\n            END IF;\n        END LOOP;\n    END IF;\n    if ( r - n > 45) THEN\n        while (doMagic) LOOP\n            findRabbits;\n        END LOOP;\n    END IF;\n    BEGIN\n        doSomethingDangerous();\n    EXCEPTION WHEN FooException THEN\n        makeAmends;\n        BEGIN\n            dontDoItAgain;\n        EXCEPTION\n        WHEN OTHERS THEN\n            log_problem;\n        END;\n    END;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_TooManyFields",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Too Many Fields in Classes or Packages",
    "description" : "Detects classes or packages that have too many fields, which can make the code unwieldy and hard to maintain.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nClasses that have too many fields can become unwieldy and could be redesigned to have fewer fields,\npossibly through grouping related fields in new objects.  For example, a class with individual\ncity/state/zip fields could park them within a single Address field.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE pkg_too_many_fields AS\n    C_CHAR_A CONSTANT CHAR(1 CHAR) := 'A';\n    C_CHAR_B CONSTANT CHAR(1 CHAR) := 'B';\n    ...\n    C_CHAR_Z CONSTANT CHAR(1 CHAR) := 'Z';\nEND pkg_too_many_fields;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_TooManyMethods",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Too Many Methods",
    "description" : "This rule detects objects that have too many methods, suggesting that the object is overly complex and should be refactored.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nA package or type with too many methods is probably a good suspect for refactoring, in order to reduce its complexity and find a way to\nhave more fine grained objects.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "maxmethods",
        "default" : "1",
        "description" : "The method count reporting threshold"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_errorprone_TO_DATE_TO_CHAR",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using TO_DATE(TO_CHAR(variable)) to Remove Time Component",
    "description" : "Detects usage of TO_DATE(TO_CHAR(date-variable)) to remove the time component from a date, which is inefficient and error-prone.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nTO_DATE(TO_CHAR(date-variable)) used to remove time component - use TRUNC(date-variable)\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY date_utilities\nIS\n\n-- Take single parameter, relying on current default NLS date format\nFUNCTION strip_time (p_date IN DATE) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   RETURN TO_DATE(TO_CHAR(p_date));\nEND strip_time;\n\n\nEND date_utilities;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_errorprone_TO_DATEWithoutDateFormat",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid TO_DATE Without Date Format",
    "description" : "Detects usage of TO_DATE function without specifying an explicit date format, which can lead to errors due to reliance on default NLS date format.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nTO_DATE without date format- use TO_DATE(expression, date-format)\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY date_utilities\nIS\n\n-- Take single parameter, relying on current default NLS date format\nFUNCTION to_date_single_parameter (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   RETURN TO_DATE(p_date_string);\nEND to_date_single_parameter ;\n\n-- Take 2 parameters, using an explicit date format string\nFUNCTION to_date_two_parameters (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2, p_format_mask IN VARCHAR2) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   TO_DATE(p_date_string, p_date_format);\nEND to_date_two_parameters;\n\n-- Take 3 parameters, using an explicit date format string and an explicit language\nFUNCTION to_date_three_parameters (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2, p_format_mask IN VARCHAR2, p_nls_language VARCHAR2 ) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   TO_DATE(p_date_string, p_format_mask, p_nls_language);\nEND to_date_three_parameters;\n\nEND date_utilities;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_errorprone_TO_TIMESTAMPWithoutDateFormat",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid TO_TIMESTAMP Without Date Format",
    "description" : "This rule flags the use of TO_TIMESTAMP function calls without specifying an explicit date format.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nTO_TIMESTAMP without date format- use TO_TIMESTAMP(expression, date-format)\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY date_utilities\nIS\n\n-- Take single parameter, relying on current default NLS date format\nFUNCTION to_timestamp_single_parameter (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   RETURN TO_TIMESTAMP(p_date_string);\nEND to_timestamp_single_parameter;\n\n-- Take 2 parameters, using an explicit date format string\nFUNCTION to_timestamp_two_parameters (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2, p_format_mask IN VARCHAR2) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   TO_TIMESTAMP(p_date_string, p_date_format);\nEND to_timestamp_two_parameters;\n\n-- Take 3 parameters, using an explicit date format string and an explicit language\nFUNCTION to_timestamp_three_parameters (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2, p_format_mask IN VARCHAR2, p_nls_language VARCHAR2 ) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   TO_TIMESTAMP(p_date_string, p_format_mask, p_nls_language);\nEND to_timestamp_three_parameters;\n\nEND date_utilities;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_pom_errorprone_InvalidDependencyTypes",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Invalid Dependency Types in Maven",
    "description" : "Detects usage of invalid dependency types in Maven dependency management which Maven ignores silently.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.4\n\nIf you use an invalid dependency type in the dependency management section, Maven doesn't fail. Instead,\nthe entry is just ignored, which might have the effect, that the wrong version of the dependency is used.\n\nThe following types are considered valid: pom, jar, maven-plugin, ejb, war, ear, rar, par.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<project...>\n  ...\n  <dependencyManagement>\n      ...\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.jboss.arquillian</groupId>\n      <artifactId>arquillian-bom</artifactId>\n      <version>${arquillian.version}</version>\n      <type>bom</type> <!-- not a valid type ! 'pom' is ! -->\n      <scope>import</scope>\n    </dependency>\n    ...\n  </dependencyManagement>\n</project>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "validTypes",
        "default" : "pom,jar,maven-plugin,ejb,war,ear,rar,par",
        "description" : "Set of valid types."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "XML"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_pom_errorprone_ProjectVersionAsDependencyVersion",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Project Version As Dependency Version",
    "description" : "This rule detects usage of ${project.version} to specify dependency versions in Maven POM files.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.4\n\nUsing that expression in dependency declarations seems like a shortcut, but it can go wrong.\nBy far the most common problem is the use of ${project.version} in a BOM or parent POM.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<project...>\n  ...\n  <dependency>\n    ...\n    <version>${project.version}</version>\n  </dependency>\n</project>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "XML"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_vf_security_VfCsrf",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "CSRF",
    "title" : "Avoid Calling Visualforce Action Upon Page Load",
    "description" : "This rule detects when a Visualforce page calls a controller action method automatically upon page load.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.6.0\n\nAvoid calling VF action upon page load as the action becomes vulnerable to CSRF.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<apex:page controller=\"AcRestActionsController\" action=\"{!csrfInitMethod}\" >\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "VisualForce"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_vf_security_VfHtmlStyleTagXss",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Enforce Correct Encoding for Expressions Within Style Tags in Visualforce",
    "description" : "Ensures that expressions within <style> tags in Visualforce pages use the correct encoding to prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.31.0\n\nChecks for the correct encoding in `&lt;style/&gt;` tags in Visualforce pages.\n\nThe rule is based on Salesforce Security's recommendation to prevent XSS in Visualforce as mentioned\non [Secure Coding Cross Site Scripting](https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.secure_coding_guide.meta/secure_coding_guide/secure_coding_cross_site_scripting.htm).\n\nIn order to avoid cross site scripting, the relevant encoding must be used in HTML tags. The rule expects\n`URLENCODING` or `JSINHTMLENCODING` for URL-based style values and any kind of encoding\n(e.g. `HTMLENCODING`) for non-url style values.\n\nSee also {% rule &quot;VfUnescapeEl&quot; %} to check escaping in other places on Visualforce pages.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<apex:page>\n    <style>\n        div {\n            background: url('{!XSSHere}'); // Potential XSS\n        }\n        div {\n            background: url('{!URLENCODE(XSSHere)}'); // correct encoding\n        }\n    </style>\n</apex:page>\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "VisualForce"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_vf_security_VfUnescapeEl",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Avoid Unescaped User Controlled Content in EL",
    "description" : "This rule detects usage of unescaped user-controlled content in Expression Language (EL) in Visualforce pages, which can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.6.0\n\nAvoid unescaped user controlled content in EL as it results in XSS.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<apex:outputText value=\"Potential XSS is {! here }\" escape=\"false\" />\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "VisualForce"
      }
    ]
  },
